WEBVTT

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:52.590
<v Ira Glass>Freddie is the general manager of a car dealership these days, but he used to be a car salesman. And he was a good one, partly because he's got what he calls the gift for the gab. His go-to move in lots of situations is to finish his sentence and then laugh, even when the sentence is bad news. Like, here he is assessing his chances of making his sales goal for the month.
<v Ira Glass>弗雷迪最近是一家汽车经销商的总经理，但他以前是一名汽车销售员。而且他是个优秀的销售，部分原因是他拥有所谓“能说会道”的天赋。他在很多场合的招牌动作是说完话后大笑，即使那句话是坏消息。比如，这里他在评估自己达成当月销售目标的机会。

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.430
<v Freddie Hoyt>I'll give it a 50/50 right now because it's early.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我现在给它打50/50，因为时间还早。

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.140
<v Freddie Hoyt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Freddie Hoyt>[笑声]

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.430
<v Ira Glass>Or this is him talking about a month when the car dealership did not make its sales goal.
<v Ira Glass>或者这是他在谈论汽车经销店没能达到销售目标的一个月。

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.220
<v Freddie Hoyt>And I'm strictly commission, so I make nothing.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我完全靠佣金，所以我什么都没赚到。

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:04.370
<v Freddie Hoyt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Freddie Hoyt>[笑声]

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:14.260
<v Ira Glass>So when Freddie is not laughing, you know it's bad. In mid-October, Freddie did not laugh much at his weekly meetings with the guys who sell cars for him.
<v Ira Glass>所以当弗雷迪不笑的时候，你就知道情况很糟。十月中旬，弗雷迪在每周与手下销售员的会议上没怎么笑过。

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:32.600
<v Freddie Hoyt>All right. Good morning. All right. In the beginning of the month, I went through the room and I told everybody where they had to be. Jason, you have 16 cars out. You need 10 more for the month for 26. I want to see you there.
<v Freddie Hoyt>好了，早上好。好了，月初我巡视了整个房间，告诉每个人他们需要达到什么目标。杰森，你卖出了16辆车。这个月还需要卖10辆才能达到26辆。我想在那看到你。

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:40.060
<v Freddie Hoyt>Bob Lundy, you have six out. You need nine more. Scotty, you're at 10 right now. You need to be at 18.
<v Freddie Hoyt>鲍勃·伦迪，你卖出了6辆。你还需要卖9辆。斯科蒂，你现在是10辆。你需要达到18辆。

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:54.020
<v Ira Glass>Last month, they didn't make their sales goal. That was September. The first week of school is always bad for car sales in the suburbs. They were supposed to sell 127 cars and trucks. They sold 82. So they have to make October.
<v Ira Glass>上个月，他们没能达到销售目标。那是九月。开学的第一周对于郊区的汽车销售来说总是很糟糕。他们本应卖掉127辆汽车和卡车，结果只卖了82辆。所以他们必须拿下十月。

00:01:54.020 --> 00:02:26.890
<v Ira Glass>And when I say have to make, this is not some sort of abstract, feel-good, "compete with the dealership down the block just for fun" kind of competition. They're part of Chrysler. And if they sell 129 cars and trucks by the end of October, Chrysler will pay them a bonus that's pretty much the difference between the dealership being in the black or being in the red for the month, somewhere between $65,000 and $85,000, depending on which models they sell. Different cars earn different amounts. If they sell 128 cars, fall just one car short, they get nothing.
<v Ira Glass>当我说是必须拿下时，这并不是某种抽象的、感觉良好的，“为了好玩而与街对面的经销店竞争”那样的竞争。他们是克莱斯勒的一部分。如果他们在十月底前卖掉129辆汽车和卡车，克莱斯勒会付给他们一笔奖金，这笔奖金几乎就是该经销店当月是盈利还是亏损的区别，大约在65,000美元到85,000美元之间，具体取决于他们卖的是什么车型。不同的车赚取的金额不同。如果他们卖出128辆车，只差一辆，他们就什么也拿不到。

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:32.760
<v Marc Brodlieb>That's the worksheet that Chrysler sends us to show us what money we can earn by how many cars we sell.
<v Marc Brodlieb>那是克莱斯勒发给我们的工作表，用来向我们展示通过销售多少辆车我们可以赚到多少钱。

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.290
<v Ira Glass>And there's a new one every month?
<v Ira Glass>每个月都有新的吗？

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.390
<v Marc Brodlieb>Every month.
<v Marc Brodlieb>每个月都有。

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:43.460
<v Ira Glass>In the middle of the month, the owner of this dealership, a sweet-faced guy named Marc Brodlieb, and Freddie, show me the document where Chrysler set their October sales goal.
<v Ira Glass>月中，这家店的老板，一个面容和蔼的家伙叫马克·布罗德利布，和弗雷迪给我看了克莱斯勒设定他们十月销售目标的文件。

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.170
<v Freddie Hoyt>No. No. This changes every month.
<v Freddie Hoyt>不，不。这每个月都在变。

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:46.130
<v Marc Brodlieb>At their whim.
<v Marc Brodlieb>随他们的意。

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:46.920
<v Freddie Hoyt>At their whim.
<v Freddie Hoyt>随他们的意。

00:02:46.920 --> 00:03:01.710
<v Marc Brodlieb>And that's another frustration. We have no idea what the number's going to be and really how they compute it. We've had numbers as high as 159. And we fight and we go crazy. It's cockamamie, if anyone knows what that means.
<v Marc Brodlieb>这也是另一个挫折。我们根本不知道数字会是多少，也不知道他们到底是怎么计算的。我们甚至遇到过高达159的数字。我们抗争，我们发疯。如果有人知道“cockamamie”（荒唐）是什么意思的话，那就是它。

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.010
<v Ira Glass>Because basically they shift around the ground rules on you.
<v Ira Glass>因为基本上他们会随意改变基本规则。

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.290
<v Marc Brodlieb>Every month.
<v Marc Brodlieb>每个月。

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:29.740
<v Ira Glass>What makes this tricky is that there are four other Jeep dealerships within 10 miles of Marc and Freddie, and Marc says that most customers will shop price at two or three of them. So to stay competitive, Marc and Freddie do what pretty much everybody else does. They set lower prices, knock hundreds of dollars off the price of the cars, sometimes sell them for less than they pay Chrysler for them, with the hope that they're going to hit their sales goal and get the bonus to make up the difference.
<v Ira Glass>这事棘手的地方在于，在马克和弗雷迪的店方圆10英里内还有四家Jeep经销商，马克说大多数客户会在其中两三家进行比价。为了保持竞争力，马克和弗雷迪做了几乎所有人都在做的事情。他们设定更低的价格，从车价中砍掉几百美元，有时甚至以低于他们付给克莱斯勒的价格卖车，希望他们能达成销售目标并获得奖金来弥补差价。

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:37.290
<v Marc Brodlieb>But there's no guarantee we will hit this number. So the pressure is huge because we have already given away the money to the consumer, using that to sell the car.
<v Marc Brodlieb>但不能保证我们能达到这个数字。所以压力巨大，因为我们已经把钱让利给消费者了，用那个来卖车。

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:42.830
<v Freddie Hoyt>So I need a big day today. I need a big day, no bull. I really need a big day today.
<v Freddie Hoyt>所以我今天需要大干一场。我需要大干一场，不吹牛。我真的需要今天大干一场。

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:52.480
<v Ira Glass>So with 12 days left to go in the month, numbers wobbly, Freddie laid it on thick during his weekly meeting with the sales team. He told them he sees them over the dealership's security cams.
<v Ira Glass>所以，在离月末还有12天的时候，数字动摇，弗雷迪在每周与销售团队的会议上施加了巨大的压力。他告诉他们，他通过经销店的监控摄像头看着他们。

00:03:52.480 --> 00:04:15.850
<v Freddie Hoyt>I look through the video. I watch you guys in there. All on your computers, going to different websites, chilling. Dude, get on higher gear. This is no joke. I've got to be at that number, or I'm telling you, I'm not going to be a nice guy. So put your nose right to the ground and come out shooting today, everybody.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我查看视频。我看着你们在里面。都在电脑上，浏览不同的网站，放松。伙计们，上档次。这不是开玩笑。我必须达到那个数字，否则我告诉你们，我不会是个好说话的人。所以每个人都给我埋头苦干，今天火力全开。

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:29.930
<v Freddie Hoyt>I want balloons in all the departments. I want the radios down on. Put the convertible tops down all over the place. I want tons of balloons in the showroom. I just don't want one balloon to a car. Balloon the whole freaking place so it looks like a circus.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我要所有部门都挂上气球。我要把广播调小。把所有地方的敞篷车顶都放下来。我想要展厅里有成吨的气球。我不想一辆车只配一个气球。把整个鬼地方都塞满气球，让它看起来像个马戏团。

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:41.550
<v Freddie Hoyt>Make it seem like we're having a monster sale and it's a party, because we've got to be at the big number by the 31st, midnight, period, no ands, ifs, and buts.
<v Freddie Hoyt>让它看起来好像我们正在举行一场盛大的特卖会，是一场派对，因为我们必须在31号午夜前达到那个大数字，期限到了，没商量，没借口。

00:04:41.550 --> 00:05:09.810
<v Ira Glass>So everybody grabs balloons. Grown men inflate and tie and decorate. It's a truism in their business, balloons sell cars. And then nobody shows up for an hour, two hours, three hours-- four hours. Saturday should be one of their busiest days, and it's empty.
<v Ira Glass>于是每个人都去抓气球。成年男人充气、系绳、装饰。这在他们的生意里是一句真理，气球能卖车。然后一个小时、两个小时、三个小时……四个小时过去了，没一个人来。周六应该是他们最忙的日子之一，但店里空空如也。

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.180
<v Brian Reed>Hey, Freddie. How's it going today so far?
<v Brian Reed>嘿，弗雷迪。今天到目前为止怎么样？

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.990
<v Freddie Hoyt>Shit. Really bad. Really slow. Really, really slow.
<v Freddie Hoyt>该死。非常糟糕。非常冷清。真的，真的非常冷清。

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:26.830
<v Ira Glass>Early afternoon, Freddie told one of our producers, Brian Reed, that it was way slower than usual. I don't know if you can catch this, but try to notice the song that is playing over the radio in the background as they talk.
<v Ira Glass>午后，弗雷迪告诉我们的制片人布莱恩·里德，生意比平时慢多了。我不知道你们能否听出来，但试着注意他们在交谈时背景广播里播放的歌。

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.730
<v Brian Reed>What do you think it is? What's going on?
<v Brian Reed>你觉得那是什么歌？发生了什么？

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:37.270
<v Freddie Hoyt>Not a clue. With all the advertisings out there and everything else, we should be swamped. I'm stressing. Oh, I'm stressing.
<v Freddie Hoyt>一点头绪都没有。有那么多广告和其它一切，我们本该忙得不可开交。我压力很大。噢，我压力很大。

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:37.490
<v Freddie Hoyt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Freddie Hoyt>[笑声]

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.280
<v Freddie Hoyt>[MUSIC - "DON'T WORRY BE HAPPY" BY BOBBY MCFERRIN]
<v Freddie Hoyt>[音乐 - 鲍比·麦克菲林的《Don't Worry Be Happy》]

00:05:39.280 --> 00:06:07.950
<v Ira Glass>Marc, who owns this dealership, and Freddie, who runs it, both know that most people do not trust car dealers. Most people think that they're ripping us off, throwing on extra charges, milking us for every dime. And they say, if you and I only knew the reality, we would see that, in fact, the dealer is not making a killing off us. In fact, the dealer is often squeaking by with a very thin profit margin. They say it is all very different from what people think. And to prove their point, they let us watch.
<v Ira Glass>拥有这家店的马克和管理它的弗雷迪都知道，大多数人并不信任汽车经销商。大多数人认为他们在宰我们，额外收费，榨取我们的每一分钱。他们说，如果你我知道真相，就会发现实际上经销商并没有从我们身上大捞一笔。事实上，经销商往往利润空间极薄，勉强维持。他们说一切与人们想象的完全不同。为了证明这一点，他们让我们旁观。

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:49.140
<v Ira Glass>A bunch of us from our radio show, we recorded them as they headed towards the end of the month, trying to make their goal, trying to sell 129 cars, which, you know, you would think that would be very straightforward, very businesslike. After all, new cars and trucks, they're 15% of all retail sales. But if what we saw was typical, what that means is that hundreds of billions of dollars in economic activity and nearly 2 million jobs exist thanks only to millions of messy seat-of-the-pants deals, many of which barely come together, as salesmen sweat out their end-of-the-month quotas. It is way more chaotic than we expected.
<v Ira Glass>我们广播节目的几个人记录了他们走向月末的过程，努力达成目标，试图卖出129辆车，你知道，你本以为这会是非常直接、非常公事公办的。毕竟，新车和卡车占了所有零售额的15%。但如果我们所见是典型的，这意味着数千亿美元的经济活动和近200万个工作岗位仅归功于数百万笔混乱的、临时的交易，其中许多交易几乎无法达成，正如销售员们为月末指标而流汗。这比我们预期的混乱得多。

00:06:49.140 --> 00:07:05.060
<v Ira Glass>And we saw things, it's true, we think will surprise a lot of car buyers. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. Today, one car dealership tries to sell 129 cars. We get to know the resolute men and women who try to pull that off.
<v Ira Glass>而且我们看到的东西，没错，我们认为会令很多购车者感到惊讶。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。今天，一家汽车经销商试图卖出129辆车。我们要了解那些试图完成这一壮举的坚定的男男女女。

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:14.610
<v Ira Glass>Quick note, this show is a rerun. Everything you'll hear in this hour all happened in October a couple years back. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>简短说明，本节目是重播。你在这一个小时内听到的所有内容都发生在几年前的十月。请继续收听。

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:44.460
<v Ira Glass>So I wish I could pretend that we got interested in this subject at our radio show because we realized what an important part of the economy car sales are. And I wish I could pretend that we did a huge survey to find the most typical dealership out there. But in fact, we got interested in this topic and this particular dealership-- Town & Country Jeep Chrysler Dodge Ram is what it's called, in Levittown, New York, on Long Island-- when one of our coworkers here at the radio show, Robyn Semien, bought a used car there.
<v Ira Glass>我希望我能假装我们对这个主题感兴趣是因为我们意识到汽车销售是经济中多么重要的一部分。我也希望我能假装我们做了一项大规模调查来寻找最典型的经销店。但事实上，我们对这个话题和这家特定的经销店——名为Town & Country Jeep Chrysler Dodge Ram，位于纽约长岛的莱维敦——感兴趣，是因为我们广播节目的同事罗宾·塞米恩在那里买了一辆二手车。

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:57.500
<v Ira Glass>Here's how that happened. A friend of hers who knows all about cars told her that the only vehicle that she should even consider for her young family is the Honda Pilot. So she went looking online. She saw one listed on cars.com. It was at Town & Country.
<v Ira Glass>事情是这样的。她的一位非常懂车的朋友告诉她，她唯一应该考虑给年轻家庭买的交通工具就是本田Pilot。于是她上网查找。她看到cars.com上列出了一辆。就在Town & Country。

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:10.630
<v Ira Glass>And she called. And she had no intention at all of going to look at the car. But a salesman convinced her, oh, you know what? You should come out today, right today. Talked her into it, which meant an hour-long ride on a train from Brooklyn with her husband and her four-year-old.
<v Ira Glass>她打了电话。她根本没打算去看车。但一位销售员说服了她，噢，你知道吗？你应该今天就出来，就今天。说服了她，这意味着她要和丈夫以及四岁的孩子从布鲁克林坐一个小时的火车。

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:24.740
<v Ira Glass>And then once she got there, they upsold her to a newer, more expensive vehicle, a Jeep Liberty, which she drove off the lot 24 hours later. Robyn told me this story in the car in question on our first ride out to the dealership.
<v Ira Glass>等她到了那里，他们向她推销了一辆更新、更贵的车，一辆Jeep Liberty，她在24小时后就把车开了回去。在我们第一次去经销店的车里，罗宾告诉了我这个故事。

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:39.570
<v Robyn Semien>Yeah. I mean, the Honda Pilot, I think, is across the board, less problems, lasts longer, better gas mileage.
<v Robyn Semien>是的。我是说，本田Pilot，我认为，总的来说，问题较少，寿命更长，油耗更好。

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:39.750
<v Robyn Semien>[LAUGHTER]
<v Robyn Semien>[笑声]

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:46.050
<v Robyn Semien>I know. I know. I know. I know, but I love this car.
<v Robyn Semien>我知道。我知道。我知道。我知道，但我就是喜欢这辆车。

00:08:46.050 --> 00:09:03.970
<v Ira Glass>You got that? These people are such good salesmen, not only did she pay more, not only did they put her into a Jeep, which meant that, since they are a Jeep dealer, they could sell her a warranty, which they also did for a few thousand dollars, she came out of this loving her car and loving them. How did they do this?
<v Ira Glass>听到了吗？这些人是如此优秀的销售员，不仅她付了更多的钱，不仅他们让她买了一辆Jeep，这意味着，既然他们是Jeep经销商，他们可以卖给她一份保修，他们也确实这么做了，花了她几千美元，她从这里出来时还爱上了她的车，也爱上了他们。他们是怎么做到的？

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:24.620
<v Ira Glass>Well, before we go any further, I think I should give you a quick primer on the basic setup and cast of characters at this dealership. New cars are sold at Town & Country-- and we're going to be spending most of our time with new cars today-- by seven men and one woman, who, by the way, constantly make fun of each other in this raunchy way that we cannot play you over the radio. Just know that that's happening.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，在我们继续之前，我想先给你简要介绍一下这家经销店的基本设置和人物阵容。Town & Country销售新车——今天我们大部分时间将花在新车上——的是七名男性和一名女性，顺便说一下，他们不断以一种我们无法在广播中播放的下流方式互相取笑。只要知道有这回事就行。

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:41.810
<v Ira Glass>Plus, there are two guys in the finance department. And then, overseeing all of them, there are the general manager, Freddie, who you already met, and then, out on the showroom floor all day, every day, are two managers who you're going to be hearing from a lot over the course of this hour. These are Mike Perez and Sal Lanzilotta.
<v Ira Glass>此外，财务部有两个人。然后，监督他们所有人的是总经理弗雷迪，你已经见过了，然后，每天整天在展厅地板上的，是你在这一小时内会听到很多的两位经理。他们是迈克·佩雷斯和萨尔·兰齐洛塔。

00:09:41.810 --> 00:10:07.110
<v Ira Glass>Of the two, Sal is the more complicated figure. Balding, with a gray goatee, he's got this vibe that is halfway between Ben Kingsley in Gandhi and the laconic hitman Mike from Breaking Bad. When Sal gets up to speak at the weekly sales meetings-- the meeting, by the way, is in the middle of the showroom-- he paces around a red convertible as he talks, reminding everybody of the basic tenets of car sales.
<v Ira Glass>两人中，萨尔是一个更复杂的人物。秃顶，留着灰色山羊胡，他身上有一种介于电影《甘地传》中的本·金斯利和《绝命毒师》中那种冷漠杀手迈克之间的气质。当萨尔在每周销售会议上发言时——顺便说一下，会议是在展厅中间举行的——他一边说话一边围着一辆红色敞篷车踱步，提醒大家汽车销售的基本准则。

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:19.380
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Customer says they're not ready to buy a car. They're all not ready to buy a car. Let's go over it again. They're in a car dealership.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>客户说他们还没准备好买车。他们都没准备好买车。让我们再过一遍。他们在一家汽车经销店。

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:38.800
<v Sal Lanzilotta>They got in their car, drove through hell to get here, looked for a parking spot for 10 minutes, parked, got out of the car, and walked into a car dealer. Not because the coffee's good. We went over this. Because the coffee here is not good. They came here because we sell cars, and they want to buy one.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>他们坐上车，经历了地狱才来到这里，找了10分钟停车位，停车，下车，走进一家汽车经销商。不是因为咖啡好喝。我们说过了。因为这里的咖啡不好喝。他们来这里是因为我们卖车，而他们想买一辆。

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:47.550
<v Ira Glass>Nothing's changed in a hundred years of selling cars, he tells them at one meeting. The customer wants to pay as little as possible. We want to make as much as we can.
<v Ira Glass>卖车一百年来什么都没变，他在一次会议上告诉他们。客户想付尽可能少的钱。我们想赚尽可能多的钱。

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:51.310
<v Sal Lanzilotta>You guys know the job, right? You've all done it before.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>你们都知道这份工作，对吧？你们以前都做过。

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:52.240
<v Freddie Hoyt>Be aggressive.
<v Freddie Hoyt>要积极。

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.620
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Be aggressive. ABC, Bob. What's ABC?
<v Sal Lanzilotta>要积极。ABC，鲍勃。ABC是什么？

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:00.390
<v Bob Tantillo>Always Be Closing.
<v Bob Tantillo>始终保持成交（Always Be Closing）。

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.250
<v Marc Brodlieb>Always be closing.
<v Marc Brodlieb>始终保持成交。

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:16.020
<v Ira Glass>OK. So these guys, the managers, Sal and Mike, sit near the front door of the showroom at this elevated platform that everybody calls the podium or the desk, and they oversee all the terms of all the deals.
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以这些家伙，经理萨尔和迈克，坐在展厅前门附近那个被称为讲台或服务台的高台上，他们监督所有交易的所有条款。

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:28.080
<v Ira Glass>And if you've ever bought a car, maybe you've wondered what really happens when the salesman leaves you and they go back and they talk to their manager, they talk to the boss. What do they do? Well, what they do is, we were told, they load their lips. Here's Freddie.
<v Ira Glass>如果你曾经买过车，也许你好奇过当销售员离开你，回到后面去和他们的经理、和老板交谈时，到底发生了什么。他们做了什么？好吧，我们被告知，他们是在“装填嘴唇”（向销售员授意）。这是弗雷迪。

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:35.780
<v Freddie Hoyt>We tell them what to say to the customer. And we load their lips, send it back to the customer, and see where we can commit.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我们告诉他们该对客户说什么。然后装填他们的嘴唇，把他们送回客户那里，看看我们能达成什么。

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:47.860
<v Ira Glass>So for instance, when one of the salesmen wants to get a woman to bring her husband in today to test drive the car that she's chosen, they huddle at the desk, a bunch of them, and Freddie starts to improv some lines for the salesman to say.
<v Ira Glass>所以，例如，当其中一位销售员想让一位女士今天就把她丈夫叫过来试驾她选中的车时，他们一群人挤在服务台，弗雷迪开始即兴创作一些台词让销售员去说。

00:11:47.860 --> 00:12:03.070
<v Freddie Hoyt>Listen, if you can take this car by Monday, what happens-- if I can. I'm not sure if I can do it. I have money for the weekend that I'm giving away from Chrysler. As long as you purchase it by tomorrow and pick it up by Monday, you win.
<v Freddie Hoyt>听着，如果你能在周一前提车，会发生什么——如果我能的话。我不确定我能不能做到。我手里有周末的钱，是克莱斯勒给的优惠。只要你在明天前购买，周一前提车，你就赢了。

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.370
<v Ira Glass>He's totally making this up. There's no weekend money like this.
<v Ira Glass>他完全是编造的。根本没有什么周末优惠。

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:12.430
<v Freddie Hoyt>I've got extra money over the weekend through Chrysler. I want to use it. I want to give it to you. Get your husband in here.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我周末有克莱斯勒提供的额外资金。我想用它。我想给你。把你丈夫叫过来。

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.360
<v Ira Glass>And with that, the salesman heads off.
<v Ira Glass>就这样，销售员出发了。

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:21.120
<v Freddie Hoyt>That's loading the lips. You've just seen it. Loading your lips.
<v Freddie Hoyt>那就是装填嘴唇。你刚亲眼看到了。装填你的嘴唇。

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:40.600
<v Ira Glass>But before the managers can load their lips, they have to figure out what price they're going to ask for and the terms of the deal and all that. And this works differently, I think, than the way that most of us imagine is happening when we buy a car. We assume that the managers and the salesmen are a unified team, and it's us versus them.
<v Ira Glass>但在经理们装填嘴唇之前，他们必须弄清楚他们要索要的价格、交易条款等等。我想，这和我们大多数人在买车时想象的情况不同。我们假设经理和销售员是一个统一的团队，是我们对抗他们。

00:12:40.600 --> 00:13:01.670
<v Ira Glass>But in fact, managers and salespeople are often at odds with each other, and they spin information and they lie to each other. Here's Peter Possas, a very experienced salesman, who, at the time of this recording, was three cars shy of the 18 that he was supposed to sell in October. And what he's doing here is he's asking a couple what they want their monthly payments to be on a new car.
<v Ira Glass>但事实上，经理和销售员往往意见不合，他们会编造信息并互相撒谎。这是彼得·波萨斯，一位非常有经验的销售员，在录音时，他距离十月应卖出的18辆车还差3辆。他在这里做的是，他在询问一对夫妇他们希望新车的月供是多少。

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:11.980
<v Peter Possas>What if I can get you the same payment you're at now? $199 for 39 months, something like that?
<v Peter Possas>如果我能让你获得和现在一样的月供呢？39个月每月199美元，大概这样？

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:23.150
<v Ira Glass>OK, hear that? $199 a month. Then Peter marches over to talk to the managers at the desk. And listen to the number that he tells Sal that the customer wants.
<v Ira Glass>好的，听到了吗？每月199美元。然后彼得走过去和经理们谈话。听听他告诉萨尔客户想要的数字。

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.660
<v Peter Possas>She wants it to be about $150 a month.
<v Peter Possas>她希望每月大约150美元。

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:34.850
<v Ira Glass>$150. Not $199. As soon as Sal is out of earshot, Peter turns to the reporter who's trailing him, Sean Cole.
<v Ira Glass>150美元。不是199美元。一旦萨尔走远，彼得转向跟踪他的记者肖恩·科尔。

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.060
<v Peter Possas>You see what I did?
<v Peter Possas>看到我做了什么吗？

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.100
<v Sean Cole>So talk me through it.
<v Sean Cole>跟我说说看。

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.940
<v Peter Possas>You heard-- well, you saw what-- she told me she wants to be at $199 a month.
<v Peter Possas>你听到了——嗯，你看到了什么——她告诉我不想要每月199美元。

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:41.500
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>没错。

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:45.200
<v Peter Possas>But I'm telling him $150 because I've got to work him, too, at the same time I'm working her.
<v Peter Possas>但我告诉他150美元，因为我也得对付他，同时还要对付她。

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.160
<v Sean Cole>Oh, so you're going lower with him.
<v Sean Cole>噢，所以你对他说了一个更低的数字。

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:46.720
<v Peter Possas>Yeah.
<v Peter Possas>是的。

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:47.600
<v Sean Cole>I see.
<v Sean Cole>我明白了。

00:13:47.600 --> 00:14:09.890
<v Ira Glass>Why do this? Well, later, when the negotiations get tough, the manager, Sal, may ask Peter to squeeze the customers for a lower number on their trade-in, or maybe a higher price for the car, something that might be hard to talk the customer into. But Peter now has wiggle room. He knows he can push the couple up to $199 a month in payments because they already said yes to it. He can make up the difference there.
<v Ira Glass>为什么这么做？好吧，后来，当谈判变得艰难时，经理萨尔可能会要求彼得挤压客户，让他们降低以旧换新的价格，或者要求更高的车价，有些事情可能很难说服客户。但彼得现在有了回旋余地。他知道他可以把这对夫妇的月供推高到199美元，因为他们已经答应过这个数字。他可以在那里补回差价。

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:42.500
<v Ira Glass>In fact, as the deal proceeds, it runs into a problem. The desk, the couple, and the salesman cannot come to an agreement about how much the dealership should pay the couple for their trade-in. Sal says it is worth one price. The customers say that another Jeep dealer offered them a lot more than that for the trade-in. But of course, that may be a lie. There's a saying in the business-- buyers are liars. Or maybe the other dealership is lying to the customers. Maybe the other dealership told them an unrealistically high price that they never intend to pay. That happens, too. Everyone is playing everyone.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，随着交易的进行，它遇到了问题。服务台、这对夫妇和销售员无法就经销店应该为他们的旧车抵价多少达成一致。萨尔说它值一个价。客户说另一家Jeep经销商给他们的抵价高得多。当然，那可能是一个谎言。生意场上有句话——买家都是骗子。或者可能另一家经销店在对客户撒谎。也许另一家经销店告诉了他们一个不切实际的高价，而他们根本没打算支付。这种情况也会发生。每个人都在算计每个人。

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:49.520
<v Ira Glass>Sal, at the desk, tells Peter, the salesman, that the customer cannot be getting that trade-in price from the competitor. It can't be true.
<v Ira Glass>在服务台的萨尔告诉销售员彼得，客户不可能从竞争对手那里得到那个抵价。这不可能。

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:59.250
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Stop believing what the customer's telling you. That car does $16,500 at the auction. There's no way they're paying him $20,000 grand on a car that they could sell at auction for $16,500.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>别再相信客户告诉你的话了。那辆车在拍卖会上只值16,500美元。他们不可能付给他20,000美元买一辆他们在拍卖会上只能卖16,500美元的车。

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.730
<v Peter Possas>Then I guess he's a better negotiator than me.
<v Peter Possas>那我想他是一个比我更好的谈判者。

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:03.140
<v Sal Lanzilotta>I think he's just a better liar, and you're buying it.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>我想他只是一个更好的骗子，而你上当了。

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:03.820
<v Peter Possas>Yeah.
<v Peter Possas>是的。

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:05.490
<v Sal Lanzilotta>That's the difference.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>这就是区别。

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:30.420
<v Ira Glass>So to summarize, the managers and the salespeople are playing each other. The customer is playing them both. Each dealership is playing the other dealerships. And the manufacturer is playing all the dealerships, with its quotas and its incentives. Chrysler sold 140,083 cars and trucks in October. This is what it took to sell one of them.
<v Ira Glass>总结一下，经理和销售人员在互相对抗。客户在算计他们俩。每家经销店都在算计其他经销店。而制造商则用其配额和激励手段在算计所有的经销店。克莱斯勒在十月卖出了140,083辆汽车和卡车。这就是卖出其中一辆所付出的代价。

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:49.340
<v Ira Glass>Like I said earlier, several of us came and recorded at this dealership and each of us chose different salespeople to follow. Robyn Semien chose Bobby Tantillo. A warning to listeners, somebody uses a word in an offensive way in her story. We're going to leave that in because we're trying to document what really happened.
<v Ira Glass>正如我之前所说，我们几个人来到这家店录音，每个人都选择了不同的销售员跟随。罗宾·塞米恩选择了鲍比·坦蒂洛。给听众一个警告，她的故事里有人用了冒犯性的词汇。我们会保留它，因为我们试图记录下真正发生的事情。

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:56.230
<v Robyn Semien>Robert Tantillo-- Bobby T around the dealership-- is in last place. He doesn't like talking about it.
<v Robyn Semien>罗伯特·坦蒂洛——店里的鲍比·T——排在最后一名。他不喜欢谈论这个。

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.190
<v Robyn Semien>How many cars have you sold?
<v Robyn Semien>你卖了多少辆车？

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:00.190
<v Bob Tantillo>I can't keep track.
<v Bob Tantillo>我记不住。

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:04.490
<v Robyn Semien>I know someone here is keeping track.
<v Robyn Semien>我知道这里有人在记。

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:07.100
<v Bob Tantillo>Well, then you'd have to ask them.
<v Bob Tantillo>好吧，那你得问他们。

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:21.800
<v Robyn Semien>I didn't have to ask them. Freddie makes it clear in that weekly speech he gives every Saturday. Remember that meeting? He goes over everyone's individual sales, gives them new goals. Bob's there that morning, too. Freddie is keeping track.
<v Robyn Semien>我没必要问他们。弗雷迪在每个周六的演讲中说得很清楚。还记得那个会议吗？他翻阅每个人的个人销售额，给他们设定新目标。鲍勃那天早上也在场。弗雷迪在记着呢。

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:26.370
<v Freddie Hoyt>Bob T, you're at three. Thanks for stopping by.
<v Freddie Hoyt>鲍勃·T，你卖了3辆。谢谢你的光临。

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:33.530
<v Robyn Semien>Bob doesn't flinch. He sits poker-faced in a chair. A couple of guys look down or stare at their hands.
<v Robyn Semien>鲍勃没有退缩。他面无表情地坐在椅子上。有几个人低头看着手。

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:36.190
<v Freddie Hoyt>I want 15, Bob. 12 more.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我要15辆，鲍勃。还得12辆。

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:53.870
<v Robyn Semien>Bobby T's new here. Before this, he was in the wholesale food business for three decades, became a car salesman a few years ago after he retired. Bob says last month, he had the most sales for a couple weeks. But last month was last month. Freddie needs this month.
<v Robyn Semien>鲍比·T是新来的。在此之前，他在批发食品行业干了三十年，退休后几年前成了汽车销售员。鲍勃说上个月，他有两周是销售额最高的。但上个月是上个月。弗雷迪需要这个月。

00:16:53.870 --> 00:17:08.240
<v Robyn Semien>Bob says the issue is, there's just so much about selling cars that's out of his control. He says his biggest problem this month is a lack of leads. One way salesmen get leads is from the internet. Town & Country lists all the cars it has online.
<v Robyn Semien>鲍勃说问题在于，卖车这件事有太多他无法控制的东西。他说他这个月最大的问题是缺乏潜在客户。销售员获取潜在客户的一种方式是通过互联网。Town & Country在线列出了所有的车。

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:31.270
<v Robyn Semien>When people see one they like, they usually call before they come in to make sure the car is really there. But those calls go to guys in the back, in the internet department. Then when the customers show up at the store, they meet whoever they talk to on the phone, and one of the bosses at the desk hands them over to a real salesperson, like Bob. Except that Bob says he's never been handed one of those leads.
<v Robyn Semien>当人们看到喜欢的车时，他们通常会在来之前打电话以确保车真的在。但那些电话转到了后台的互联网部门。然后当客户出现在店里时，他们会见到在电话里谈话的人，服务台的老板之一会把他们转给真正的销售员，比如鲍勃。除了鲍勃说他从未被转接过那些潜在客户。

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:39.950
<v Bob Tantillo>They're going to give it to a guy that they know is-- that's been here. And they feed him the deal. That's pretty much how it works.
<v Bob Tantillo>他们会把它给一个他们认识的——一直在这儿的人。然后他们把交易喂给他。差不多就是这么工作的。

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:42.070
<v Robyn Semien>You have to really be tight with the desk.
<v Robyn Semien>你必须和后台的关系非常紧密才行。

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:42.710
<v Bob Tantillo>Yeah.
<v Bob Tantillo>是的。

00:17:42.710 --> 00:18:03.650
<v Robyn Semien>Bob's not tight with the desk. But Freddie, in an attempt to help out, gives Bob exactly what he wants-- his first internet lead. He tells Bob, go on back, get Dan. Dan works in the internet office, so these words-- "go get Dan"-- that's the signal for taking over an internet lead.
<v Robyn Semien>鲍勃和后台关系不紧密。但弗雷迪为了帮忙，给了鲍勃他想要的——他的第一个互联网潜在客户。他告诉鲍勃，去后面，找丹。丹在互联网办公室工作，所以这些话——“去把丹找来”——就是接手互联网潜在客户的信号。

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:25.310
<v Robyn Semien>Freddie raises his eyebrows at Bob and nods his head slowly like, you got that? Bob misunderstands the cue. He gets Dan from the internet office, who comes up to the desk to greet the customers and hand them off to Bob, who has suddenly disappeared. No one knows where he went, so Freddie gives the customers to another salesman. A bewildered Freddie calls over to Dan.
<v Robyn Semien>弗雷迪向鲍勃挑眉并缓慢地点点头，像在说，听懂了吗？鲍勃误解了暗示。他从互联网办公室找来丹，丹走上服务台去迎接客户并将他们转手交给鲍勃，但鲍勃突然消失了。没人知道他去了哪，所以弗雷迪把客户给了另一个销售员。困惑的弗雷迪对着丹喊道。

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:29.560
<v Freddie Hoyt>And I sent Bob T over there to get you or whatever and then come back. Where'd he go?
<v Freddie Hoyt>我派鲍勃·T去那边找你或者什么的，然后回来。他去哪了？

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.190
<v Dan>He buzzed. So I said, you busy? Come on. He kept going to the office.
<v Dan>他按了蜂鸣器。所以我说，你忙吗？来吧。他一直往办公室走。

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:34.650
<v Freddie Hoyt>He's like retarded. He's retarded.
<v Freddie Hoyt>他像个弱智。他是个弱智。

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:35.750
<v Dan>He came over to me.
<v Dan>他来到我这。

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.460
<v Freddie Hoyt>I know. I told him, go get Dan. Come right back.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我知道。我告诉他，去把丹找来。马上回来。

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:40.400
<v Robyn Semien>Bob walks up minutes later.
<v Robyn Semien>鲍勃几分钟后走了上来。

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.170
<v Freddie Hoyt>The whole idea was to get Dan and come back and then take over the sale. That's what Dan does.
<v Freddie Hoyt>整个想法是去找丹，然后回来，然后接管销售。那就是丹做的。

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:46.480
<v Bob Tantillo>I thought you wanted me to get him. That's what I thought.
<v Bob Tantillo>我以为你想让我去找他。我就是这么想的。

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:54.140
<v Freddie Hoyt>Well, that's what Dan does. He comes in. He gets the sale, and then he gives it to the salesman. So instead, now Manny got it.
<v Freddie Hoyt>好吧，那就是丹做的。他进来。他拿到销售，然后给销售员。所以现在，曼尼拿到了。

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.050
<v Bob Tantillo>I'm sorry. I was talking to Chris about the [INAUDIBLE].
<v Bob Tantillo>对不起。我正在和克里斯谈论那个[听不清]。

00:18:56.050 --> 00:19:00.010
<v Freddie Hoyt>Yeah, exactly. Three cars out. I would.
<v Freddie Hoyt>是的，确实。三辆车出去了。我会的。

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:20.220
<v Robyn Semien>It's hard to watch someone with a lot of experience berate someone with much less experience for not knowing better. There's a line from the movie Glengarry Glen Ross. "You never open your mouth till you know what the shot is." When you don't know the shot, it's not like you don't want to know the shot. Bob doesn't know the shot. But he's trying.
<v Robyn Semien>看着一个经验丰富的人指责一个经验少得多的人因为不知道窍门而责备他是件很难受的事。电影《拜金一族》里有一句台词：“不到你知道窍门时，不要开口。”当你不知道窍门时，并不是你不想知道。鲍勃不知道窍门。但他正在尝试。

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:31.130
<v Bob Tantillo>He didn't say, there's a customer here. Take care of the customer. He said, go get Dan. So I went and got Dan. I was supposed to know what he's thinking.
<v Bob Tantillo>他没说，这里有位客户。照顾好客户。他说，去把丹找来。所以我去把丹找来了。我本来应该知道他在想什么。

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:41.710
<v Bob Tantillo>It's hard. You get bullied. You get pushed around. But you let it go, move on. It's a tough job, and it's not for everybody. I told you that.
<v Bob Tantillo>这很难。你会受到欺负。你会到处碰壁。但你让它过去，继续前进。这是一份艰难的工作，不是每个人都适合。我告诉过你。

00:19:41.710 --> 00:20:01.220
<v Bob Tantillo>You've got to be thick-skinned. You've got to be able to take a lot, absorb a lot. He said, well, now we gave it to the other guy. OK. What do you want me to say?
<v Bob Tantillo>你必须脸皮厚。你必须能够承受很多，吸收很多。他说，好吧，现在我们给了别人。OK。你想让我说什么？

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:25.530
<v Ira Glass>So there actually is a place at Town & Country where they keep score of who has sold what. It's in Freddie's office, the general manager's office. It's a whiteboard with each person's sales for the month to date on it. Every car or truck that they've sold is represented by a little magnetic rectangle that's roughly the size of a 9-volt battery. Everybody calls these chips. And the different colors of the chips stand for different models of cars and trucks.
<v Ira Glass>所以Town & Country确实有一个地方记录着谁卖了什么。就在弗雷迪的办公室，总经理办公室。那是一块白板，上面有每个人当月到目前为止的销售额。他们卖出的每一辆车或卡车都用一个大约9伏电池大小的小磁矩形表示。每个人都称这些为“筹码”。筹码的不同颜色代表汽车和卡车的不同型号。

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:41.540
<v Ira Glass>And throughout the day, salesmen come and they hover around the whiteboard, seeing where everybody stands. And generally, everybody is shooting for at least 15 sales a month. At 15 sales, your commission for the next month jumps from 20% to 30%.
<v Ira Glass>整天都有销售员围着白板，看每个人处于什么位置。总的来说，每个人都在争取每月至少销售15辆。销售15辆时，你下个月的佣金会从20%跳升到30%。

00:20:41.540 --> 00:21:02.920
<v Ira Glass>There's also a bonus of $600 when you hit 10 cars, $250 if you sell seven cars in the first two weeks of the month. There's money you get directly from Chrysler, and that could be anywhere between $50 and $250 per car. Mid-level salesmen here and Town & Country make around $60,000 a year. Top performers are closer to $100,000. Most of that is commissions and bonuses.
<v Ira Glass>销售10辆时还有600美元的奖金，如果在月初两周内卖出7辆则有250美元。有从克莱斯勒直接获得的钱，每辆车可能在50到250美元之间。Town & Country的中级销售员一年大约赚60,000美元。顶尖业绩者接近100,000美元。大部分是佣金和奖金。

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:10.240
<v Ira Glass>The number one salesman at Town & Country is always the same. Some months, he has twice as many sales as whoever's in second place.
<v Ira Glass>Town & Country的头号销售员总是同一个人。有些月份，他的销售额是第二名的两倍。

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:13.750
<v Peter Possas>I've been doing this 28 years. This guy is one of the best I've ever seen.
<v Peter Possas>我干这一行28年了。这家伙是我见过的最好的之一。

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:17.450
<v Ira Glass>That's salesman Peter Possas standing at the board. It's the middle of the month.
<v Ira Glass>那是销售员彼得·波萨斯站在板前。那是月中。

00:21:17.450 --> 00:21:27.990
<v Peter Possas>He's got 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8-- 15, two pending. 17 cars. Unbelievable. This guy's a born salesperson.
<v Peter Possas>他有1，2，3，4，5，6，7，8——15辆，两辆在处理中。17辆车。令人难以置信。这家伙是天生的销售员。

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:39.960
<v Ira Glass>All the veteran sales guys at Town & Country say these things about this guy. He also happens to be the youngest salesman of dealership. He's 28. Jason Mascia. Reporter Sean Cole followed him around.
<v Ira Glass>Town & Country所有的资深销售员都这样评价这家伙。他恰好也是店里最年轻的销售员。他28岁。杰森·马西亚。记者肖恩·科尔跟随他四处走动。

00:21:39.960 --> 00:22:01.230
<v Sean Cole>Jason stands out from everybody else on the sales floor in pretty much every way. He's taller than everybody else and way more focused on his appearance-- always a full suit and tie, double Windsor knot, gelled hair. He's handsome. And he isn't just the best seller at Town & Country. He's one of the best sellers of Jeeps, Chryslers, Dodges, and Rams in America.
<v Sean Cole>杰森在销售大厅里几乎在各方面都引人注目。他比谁都高，而且对自己的外表非常注重——永远是全套西装打领带，双温莎结，抹发胶。他很帅。而且他不仅是Town & Country最好的销售员。他也是美国最好的Jeep、克莱斯勒、道奇和Ram销售员之一。

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:06.600
<v Jason Mascia>So I'm going to go on my login here. And then, boom.
<v Jason Mascia>所以我登录我的账号。然后，砰。

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:11.340
<v Sean Cole>He showed me this internal Chrysler company website that ranks all the salespeople.
<v Sean Cole>他给我看了这个克莱斯勒内部公司网站，对所有销售人员进行排名。

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:19.540
<v Jason Mascia>I'm talking thousands of people. You see this national number? There is 29,000 salespeople in the whole entire country.
<v Jason Mascia>我在谈论成千上万的人。你看到这个全国数字了吗？全国共有29,000名销售员。

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:20.710
<v Sean Cole>And you're--
<v Sean Cole>而你是——

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:21.770
<v Jason Mascia>I'm 108.
<v Jason Mascia>我是第108名。

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:25.090
<v Sean Cole>108 out of 29,000.
<v Sean Cole>29,000名中的第108名。

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:26.560
<v Sean Cole>You have a shocked look on your face.
<v Sean Cole>你脸上露出震惊的神情。

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:32.900
<v Jason Mascia>Yeah, because I look at it and I say, that's freaking crazy. You know what I'm saying? You would think a guy that's in this business for 15 to 20 years should be doing that stuff.
<v Jason Mascia>是的，因为我看着它说，这真是太疯狂了。你知道我在说什么吗？你会认为一个在这个行业干了15到20年的人才应该做到这一点。

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:47.020
<v Sean Cole>Jason's barely been in this business for four years. When I met him in the last weekend of October, he had already outsold everybody for the month, including a guy who's been doing this since Jason was a toddler. But he says he's not competing with the others. He's competing with himself.
<v Sean Cole>杰森进入这个行业才刚满四年。当我在十月的最后一个周末见到他时，他已经领先所有人，包括一个杰森还是蹒跚学步的孩子时就一直在干这一行的人。但他说他没有和别人竞争。他在和自己竞争。

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:56.950
<v Jason Mascia>I'm trying to excel. I have my own goals that nobody would even know about. You know what I mean? So I'm like my own little celebrity in my own little body.
<v Jason Mascia>我在努力超越。我有自己没人知道的目标。你懂吗？所以我像我自己身体里的小名人。

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:13.480
<v Sean Cole>As we're sitting there talking, he pulls out a piece of paper and starts writing down everything he's saying as he says it. He always does this. I have 10 pages of notes from this interview-- his notes.
<v Sean Cole>当我们坐在那里交谈时，他拿出一张纸，开始写下他说的每一句话。他总是这样。我有这次采访的10页笔记——他的笔记。

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:24.660
<v Sean Cole>So most of the salespeople at Town & Country are shooting for 15 to 20 sales in a month. That's considered a solid performance. And Jason is not satisfied with a solid performance.
<v Sean Cole>所以Town & Country的大多数销售员都在争取每月15到20辆的销售额。那被认为是一份扎实的业绩。而杰森对扎实的业绩并不满足。

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:26.550
<v Jason Mascia>There's a number that I really want to hit.
<v Jason Mascia>有一个数字我真的想达到。

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.610
<v Sean Cole>He writes down that number and circles it and then moves on.
<v Sean Cole>他写下那个数字并圈起来，然后继续。

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:33.210
<v Jason Mascia>And I would love to just achieve-- I have achieved 30 cars several times.
<v Jason Mascia>我很乐意达成——我曾多次达成30辆车。

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:37.820
<v Sean Cole>Writes down 30, underlined "several." That is, 30 cars a month.
<v Sean Cole>写下30，在“多次”下面划线。也就是说，每月30辆。

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:43.950
<v Jason Mascia>All right? So I've done that enough to say I could do it. Now I'm trickling--
<v Jason Mascia>好了吧？所以我做过足够多次，可以说我能做到。现在我正在朝着——

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.860
<v Sean Cole>Writes down 30, plus sign.
<v Sean Cole>写下30，加号。

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:46.960
<v Jason Mascia>--30-plus.
<v Jason Mascia>——30以上。

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:53.370
<v Sean Cole>For October, hoping to finish the month in the mid-30s somewhere. But Jason's own personal Olympic goal?
<v Sean Cole>对于十月，希望能以30多辆结束这个月。但杰森个人的奥林匹克目标呢？

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:55.230
<v Sean Cole>Say what this number is that you wrote down.
<v Sean Cole>说说你写下的这个数字是什么。

00:23:55.230 --> 00:24:12.120
<v Jason Mascia>This is 40. 40's a big number. You know what I mean? If you could hit 40 cars, that's more than a car per day. That's sick. That's crazy, especially when you're selling cars. You know what I mean? It's not like I'm selling suits.
<v Jason Mascia>这是40。40是一个大数字。你知道吗？如果你能达到40辆，那超过一天一辆车。这太变态了。这太疯狂了，特别是当你卖车的时候。你知道吗？又不是卖西装。

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:35.240
<v Sean Cole>What took me a while to understand is how Jason sells so many cars, or what he's doing differently than everybody else. And we asked pretty much everybody else this question, but their answers were kind of generic. He's a good-looking kid, good personality, got the gift of the gab. Even Jason sort of defaults to truisms when you ask him what his secret is. Says he treats every customer like he would want to be treated.
<v Sean Cole>我花了一段时间才理解杰森是怎么卖出这么多车的，或者他做得和别人有什么不同。我们几乎问了其他人这个问题，但他们的回答都很一般。他是个帅小伙，性格好，能说会道。当你问他秘诀时，即使是杰森也倾向于陈词滥调。说他对待每个客户就像希望被对待的那样。

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:47.450
<v Sean Cole>But as much as you can figure out anybody in less than a week, I think Jason's success is due to three basic attributes, three characteristics he has that have propelled him to the top. So I'll go through those now.
<v Sean Cole>但在不到一周内能看透任何人的情况下，我认为杰森的成功归功于三个基本属性，他拥有的三个推他到顶峰的特征。我现在就讲讲这些。

00:24:47.450 --> 00:25:11.310
<v Sean Cole>Number one, constructive delusion. Now, Jason knows that a certain percentage of customers, depending on the month, will walk away from him without buying a car. Of course that happens. And yet, paradoxically, he enters every negotiation full in the knowledge that it will not happen. As we're sitting there, he drew a picture of a very lopsided hourglass with a line across the skinny part.
<v Sean Cole>第一，建设性错觉。现在，杰森知道，根据月份不同，一定比例的客户会不买车就离开他。这当然会发生。然而，自相矛盾的是，他带着它绝不会发生的信念进入每一次谈判。当我们坐在那里时，他画了一个非常不对称的沙漏，在细的地方画了一条线。

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:16.840
<v Jason Mascia>You know, are you looking at it half empty or half full? You know what I mean? Are you optimistic or are you-- what's the other word?
<v Jason Mascia>你是觉得它半空还是半满？你知道我在说什么吗？你是乐观主义者还是——另一个词是什么？

00:25:16.840 --> 00:25:18.040
<v Sean Cole>Pessimistic.
<v Sean Cole>悲观主义者。

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:19.480
<v Jason Mascia>Pessimistic.
<v Jason Mascia>悲观主义者。

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:24.000
<v Sean Cole>To Jason, the glass is not half empty, and it is not half full.
<v Sean Cole>对杰森来说，杯子既不是半空，也不是半满。

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:25.690
<v Jason Mascia>Me, I'm always at 100.
<v Jason Mascia>我，我总是处于100%。

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:28.330
<v Sean Cole>It is 100% full.
<v Sean Cole>它是100%满的。

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:30.860
<v Jason Mascia>Yeah, it's full. And you're like, what the hell?
<v Jason Mascia>是的，它是满的。你会说，搞什么鬼？

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:32.970
<v Sean Cole>No, it's not. There's only half of the water there. No, no, no.
<v Sean Cole>不，不是。里面只有一半水。不，不，不。

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:34.670
<v Jason Mascia>No, it's full to me.
<v Jason Mascia>不，对我来说它是满的。

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:46.220
<v Sean Cole>But for someone who barely possesses the word pessimistic in his vocabulary, that's not always how he seems on the sales floor. More than any of the other sellers, Jason's anxious.
<v Sean Cole>但对于一个词汇表里几乎没有“悲观主义者”这个词的人来说，他在销售大厅看起来并不总是这样。杰森比其他销售员更焦虑。

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:51.060
<v Jason Mascia>I'm going crazy right now. I'm biting my nails. Biting my nails off because I'm stressed.
<v Jason Mascia>我现在快疯了。我在咬指甲。咬指甲，因为我压力很大。

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:58.560
<v Sean Cole>Biting his nails, hovering around the manager's desk. It's like the dealership making its quota is personal to him.
<v Sean Cole>咬着指甲，在经理服务台周围徘徊。就像经销店完成配额对他来说是私事一样。

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:16.880
<v Sean Cole>Jason-- and this is number two on our list of attributes. Jason doesn't just want to sell cars. Jason needs to sell cars. Case in point, I watched him work this one deal, a lease on a Jeep Liberty. He played it totally cool at first. But when the haggling started, it was like someone had clamped a set of jumper cables to his ankle.
<v Sean Cole>杰森——这是我们属性列表上的第二条。杰森不仅想卖车。杰森需要卖车。以他为例，我看着他处理这一单交易，一辆Jeep Liberty的租赁。起初他表现得完全冷静。但当讨价还价开始时，就像有人在他的脚踝上夹了一根跳线。

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:25.510
<v Jason Mascia>And between 375 and 329 is 46 times 39 is another 1,700. But you've got to put the value point on it. You now have a sunroof.
<v Jason Mascia>375到329之间是46，乘以39是1,700。但你必须加上价值点。你现在有了一个天窗。

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:35.780
<v Sean Cole>The guy insists for an hour that he can't leave a deposit without talking to his wife. And then he leaves a deposit. Jason wins.
<v Sean Cole>那个人坚持了一个小时，说如果不和妻子谈谈就不能留下押金。然后他留下了押金。杰森赢了。

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:37.600
<v Jason Mascia>Anthony, I really like you. I really do.
<v Jason Mascia>安东尼，我真的很喜欢你。我真的喜欢。

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:41.410
<v Sean Cole>He runs outside to chat with another customer that he's already closed.
<v Sean Cole>他跑出去和另一个他已经成交的客户聊天。

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:42.810
<v Jason Mascia>We're getting there.
<v Jason Mascia>我们快到了。

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:48.560
<v Sean Cole>And at this point, he's completely dosed up on adrenaline, jabbering like a speed freak.
<v Sean Cole>此时此刻，他完全沉浸在肾上腺素中，像个瘾君子一样喋喋不休。

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:52.790
<v Jason Mascia>I just-- I just-- I just-- I just closed one-- I just closed one that I didn't think I was going to close.
<v Jason Mascia>我刚才——我刚才——我刚才——我刚才成交了一笔——我刚才成交了一笔我觉得我不会成交的。

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:53.570
<v Speaker 1>Really?
<v Speaker 1>真的吗？

00:26:53.570 --> 00:27:11.130
<v Jason Mascia>Yeah. The guy was on the fence. He's like, my wife, my wife, my wife, my wife, my wife. I threw something at him and he was like-- and you know those intense moments, where you're like, fuck, is he going to do it? And we did it. So exciting. It doesn't matter how many cars you do. It's like the first deal of your life.
<v Jason Mascia>是的。那家伙犹豫不决。他说，我老婆，我老婆，我老婆，我老婆，我老婆。我扔给他一些东西，然后他就像——你知道那些激烈的时刻，你心里说，他妈的，他会做吗？我们做到了。太兴奋了。不管你做了多少辆车。就像你生命中的第一笔交易一样。

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:15.970
<v Sean Cole>Two hours later, he's already crashed. It's like that deal never happened.
<v Sean Cole>两个小时后，他已经崩溃了。好像那笔交易从没发生过。

00:27:15.970 --> 00:27:24.630
<v Jason Mascia>I'm struggling right now to get these deals out, man. I'm really strapped. I feel like I told you. I'm back on that feeling-- I'm back on that grind again. I feel like I need another 10 cars.
<v Jason Mascia>我现在很难把这些交易做出去，伙计。我真的很缺。我觉得我告诉过你了。我又回到了那种感觉——我又回到了那份苦差事。我觉得我还需要再卖10辆。

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:26.410
<v Sean Cole>The high is fleeting.
<v Sean Cole>快感是短暂的。

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:27.940
<v Jason Mascia>Yeah, the high's gone. I'm ready for the next dose.
<v Jason Mascia>是的，快感没了。我已经准备好下一剂了。

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:30.200
<v Sean Cole>You're ready for the next one.
<v Sean Cole>你已经准备好下一剂了。

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:34.780
<v Jason Mascia>Yeah.
<v Jason Mascia>是的。

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:48.790
<v Sean Cole>So that's two. And then the third and final attribute that I think has led Jason to being the top seller of Town & Country, ABC. Always Be Calling your girlfriend to say, baby, I just have a couple more things to take care of and then I'll be home.
<v Sean Cole>所以是两条。然后是第三条也是最后一条属性，我认为导致杰森成为Town & Country顶级销售员的原因，ABC。始终在给你女朋友打电话说，宝贝，我只需要处理几件事，然后我就回家了。

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:50.500
<v Gena>We got into an argument about it last night.
<v Gena>我们昨晚为此吵了一架。

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:51.260
<v Sean Cole>Oh, no.
<v Sean Cole>噢，不。

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:51.680
<v Gena>Yeah.
<v Gena>是的。

00:27:51.680 --> 00:28:14.400
<v Sean Cole>This is Jason's girlfriend, Gena. She stopped by to say hi to him at the shop one of the days we were there. The night before that, he had come home too late and screwed up their dinner plans. Gena says they're always running into a store five minutes before it closes. She's imposed a no-work-talk-over-dinner rule, but Jason always has just one last thing to tell her about something that happened at work.
<v Sean Cole>这是杰森的女朋友吉娜。我们在那儿的那几天，她顺便来看他。前一天晚上，他回家太晚，搞砸了他们的晚餐计划。吉娜说他们总是在商店关门前五分钟跑进去。她实施了晚餐不谈工作的规则，但杰森总有最后一件关于工作发生的事要告诉她。

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:36.300
<v Sean Cole>And they've only been together a year, so they can both still laugh about it, at least with me. But of course, working long hours can threaten everything else in your life, and the entire business of selling cars is built on working long hours, working weekends, when more customers come in, coming in on your day off, when the store needs to meet its quota. And the damage that can do is all around Jason.
<v Sean Cole>他们在一起才一年，所以至少对我来说，他们还能对此一笑置之。但当然，长时间工作会威胁你生活中的一切，整个卖车生意就是建立在长时间工作、周末工作（当更多客户进来时）、在休息日来上班（当商店需要完成配额时）的基础上的。这可能造成的损害在杰森身边随处可见。

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:43.040
<v Sean Cole>The sales guy in the cubicle next to him, Peter Possas, told me managing a sales team at a dealership in Florida is what led to his divorce.
<v Sean Cole>隔壁工位的销售员彼得·波萨斯告诉我，管理佛罗里达一家经销店的销售团队是他离婚的原因。

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:48.820
<v Peter Possas>Yeah, of course it did. I was never home. I missed all functions and stuff because I married my job.
<v Peter Possas>是的，当然是。我从没回过家。我错过了所有活动之类的，因为我和我的工作结婚了。

00:28:48.820 --> 00:29:02.870
<v Sean Cole>Manny, who sits next to Peter, is separated from his wife partly due to the business. Theresa in finance said her job caused her divorce. Sal, one of the managers you heard from before, says the job was a factor in both of his divorces.
<v Sean Cole>坐在彼得旁边的曼尼，由于生意部分原因与妻子分居了。财务部的特蕾莎说她的工作导致了她的离婚。你之前听过的经理之一萨尔说这份工作是他两次离婚的一个因素。

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:15.690
<v Sean Cole>Still, Jason and Gena wouldn't even be together if it weren't for the job. They met at the dealership when she was buying from another salesperson. And now here they are, both driving the exact same kind of car.
<v Sean Cole>不过，如果不是因为这份工作，杰森和吉娜甚至不会在一起。他们在经销店认识，当时她正从另一个销售员手里买车。现在他们都在开同一种车。

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:27.690
<v Jason Mascia>It's funny. We both got white Jeeps. We love them. She has a '12 I got a '14. And we just have a good time. And they're great trucks. They got everything you need, all the luxury, the right size, and they last forever.
<v Jason Mascia>很有趣。我们都买了白色的Jeep。我们很爱它们。她有一辆12年的，我有一辆14年的。我们玩得很开心。它们是很棒的卡车。它们拥有你需要的一切，所有的豪华，合适的尺寸，而且它们能永远开下去。

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:29.170
<v Sean Cole>It's like you're trying to sell me now.
<v Sean Cole>搞得你现在在试图卖给我一样。

00:29:29.170 --> 00:29:48.760
<v Jason Mascia>Yeah. And if you've ever been-- yeah, you're going to go out and buy a car before the end of the night. You're going to be like, Ira, you know what? I'll be back at the office in about two hours. I'm picking up my new Jeep. I mean, this product is like on fire.
<v Jason Mascia>是的。如果你曾经——是的，你会在今晚结束前去买一辆车。你会说，艾拉，你知道吗？我大约两个小时后回到办公室。我去提我的新Jeep。我是说，这个产品太火了。

00:29:48.760 --> 00:30:07.630
<v Ira Glass>In the second half of October at Town & Country, sales stay slow. And going into the last two days of the month, they still have so many cars to sell that when Freddie sees me arrive in the morning on the next to last day of the month, knowing what it is that I'm about to ask him, he starts laughing.
<v Ira Glass>在十月的下半段，Town & Country的销售依然缓慢。进入这个月的最后两天，他们还有那么多车要卖，以至于当我第二天早上到达时，知道我正要问他什么，弗雷迪开始大笑。

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:09.750
<v Freddie Hoyt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Freddie Hoyt>[笑声]

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:18.140
<v Freddie Hoyt>Oh, boy. Here we go. We're behind 16 cars for the month so far. And that's not good. That means I need 16 cars between today and tomorrow.
<v Freddie Hoyt>哦，伙计。我们来了。我们这个月落后了16辆车。这可不好。这意味着我今天和明天之间需要16辆车。

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:30.630
<v Ira Glass>Here's how hard that number is. Freddie tells me that he only expects 15 or 16 customers to show up in the store each day, which means that to get to his number, he's going to have to sell a car to every other person who walks in the door.
<v Ira Glass>这个数字有多难呢。弗雷迪告诉我，他每天只期待15或16名客户出现在店里，这意味着为了达到他的数字，他必须卖给每个走进门的一半的人一辆车。

00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:50.640
<v Ira Glass>And what my fellow radio producers and I witness over the next two days-- OK, some deals do go through, but it's almost a catalog of all the ways that a car deal can fall apart. There were the obvious ways. A young woman doesn't get approved for her car loan. A father and son promise to come back tomorrow to sign the papers and take the car. They never come back.
<v Ira Glass>而在接下来的两天里，我的广播制作人同事和我见证了——好吧，有些交易确实成功了，但这几乎是一份汽车交易可能搞砸的所有方式的目录。有显而易见的方式。一位年轻女性没有获得车贷批准。一对父子承诺第二天回来签文件提车。他们从没回来。

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:57.240
<v Ira Glass>But there turn out to be all sorts of other ways that a car deal can implode that we had never imagined or conceived. For instance, Bob T.
<v Ira Glass>但事实证明，汽车交易可能会以各种我们从未想象过或构想过的其他方式内爆。例如，鲍勃·T。

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:58.900
<v Announcer>Bob, please see Rich in Finance. Bob.
<v Announcer>鲍勃，请去找财务部的里奇。鲍勃。

00:30:58.900 --> 00:31:09.500
<v Ira Glass>You remember Bob T, the salesman with the lowest sales of anybody this month. A deal of his starts to unravel after the paperwork is signed, after the deposit is paid.
<v Ira Glass>你还记得鲍勃·T，那个本月销售额最低的销售员。他的一个交易在文书工作签完、押金付完之后开始解体。

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:19.330
<v Ira Glass>Basically, what happens is he told a couple that their new car is $2,000 cheaper than it actually is. They found out the truth while talking to Rich, the finance manager, and kind of hit the roof.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，发生的事情是他告诉一对夫妇他们的新车比实际便宜2,000美元。他们在和财务经理里奇交谈时发现了真相，火冒三丈。

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.770
<v Customer>Why am I thinking I'm getting screwed here?
<v Customer>为什么我觉得我被坑了？

00:31:21.770 --> 00:31:38.830
<v Ira Glass>Bob T was also surprised. He himself thought it was $2,000 cheaper. The finance manager, Rich, tells Bob T that Bob T should have, first, better understood which rebates the couple's qualified for. That's where the mistake was. And second, he should have explained it to the couple.
<v Ira Glass>鲍勃·T也很惊讶。他自己也以为便宜了2,000美元。财务经理里奇告诉鲍勃·T，鲍勃·T首先应该更好地理解这对夫妇有资格获得哪些回扣。错误就在那里。其次，他应该向这对夫妇解释清楚。

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:42.590
<v Rich>You left the middle out. You left the meat of the sandwich out.
<v Rich>你漏掉了中间部分。你漏掉了三明治里的肉。

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:59.010
<v Bob Tantillo>That wasn't told to me. It was that we were going to give him the same discount. That's what I understood. I'm only going by the numbers that were given to me, and I gave him the numbers, and he signed it. It started with $28,960.
<v Bob Tantillo>没人告诉我那个。这就是说我们将给他们同样的折扣。我是这么理解的。我只是照着给我的数字去做，我给了他们数字，他签了名。它从28,960美元开始。

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:03.720
<v Ira Glass>Rich manages to save this deal, but barely.
<v Ira Glass>里奇成功地挽救了这笔交易，但非常勉强。

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:22.040
<v Ira Glass>THEN AROUND 7: 00 at night on the next-to-last day of the month, one of the salesmen, Scott Froehlich, gets some weird news about one of his sales. He tells one of our producers, Jonathan Menjivar, that some customers that he had just sold a car to were now coming back to the dealership, but not to pick up their new car and take it home. No, no, no.
<v Ira Glass>然后在该月的倒数第二天晚上7点左右，销售员之一斯科蒂·弗罗利希收到了一些关于他销售的奇怪消息。他告诉我们的制作人乔纳森·门吉瓦尔，一些他刚卖出一辆车的客户现在要回到经销店，但不是为了提走他们的新车回家。不，不，不。

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:26.590
<v Scott Froehlich>They have to pick out another car because the other car was sold and nobody marked it.
<v Scott Froehlich>他们必须挑选另一辆车，因为另一辆车被卖了，没人做记号。

