<v Sean Cole>He finds the folder and learns that Stephen and Lori never got to a final final offer for his trade-in. In order to do that, the car has to go up on the lift for a full inspection in the service department, which is inexplicably closed on this, the second-to-last day of the month, when they need absolutely every sale they can get.
<v Sean Cole>他找到文件夹，了解到斯蒂芬和洛丽从来没对他的旧车抵价达成最后的最终报价。为了做到这一点，车必须放在升降机上在维修部进行全面检查，而那个维修部在此月的倒数第二天（他们需要每一笔销售的时候）莫名其妙地关闭了。

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:54.190
<v Sal Lanzilotta>They didn't call me to see if they could leave. We're going to lose this deal.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>他们没打电话给我看他们能否离开。我们会失去这笔交易。

00:36:54.190 --> 00:37:04.860
<v Sean Cole>With no inspection, there's no trade-in. And with no trade-in, there's no deal. Somebody finally does a cursory inspection on the car, without the lift, in the dark.
<v Sean Cole>没有检查就没有以旧换新。没有以旧换新就没有交易。有人终于在黑暗中，不用升降机对车进行了粗略检查。

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:18.500
<v Sean Cole>All the while, Stephen is getting more and more anxious that no one seems to know what's going on. Jason tries to get him in with the finance guy to work out the terms of the deal, but the finance guy's busy. Jason explains this to Sal, which sets Sal off again.
<v Sean Cole>一直以来，斯蒂芬越来越焦虑，因为没人知道发生了什么。杰森试图让他去财务部敲定交易条款，但财务员很忙。杰森向萨尔解释了这一点，这再次让萨尔爆发了。

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:21.050
<v Sal Lanzilotta>He needs to sit with that customer. This is a--
<v Sal Lanzilotta>他需要和那个客户坐在一起。这是一个——

00:37:21.050 --> 00:37:23.540
<v Jason Mascia>No, don't worry. Don't get excited. Don't get excited.
<v Jason Mascia>不，别担心。别激动。别激动。

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:25.360
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Dude, I'm about ready to walk the fuck out.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>伙计，我快要走了。

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:26.390
<v Jason Mascia>No, don't get--
<v Jason Mascia>不，别——

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:27.780
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Like, no bullshit.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>没有废话。

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:28.990
<v Jason Mascia>No, don't get excited.
<v Jason Mascia>不，别激动。

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.520
<v Sal Lanzilotta>OK? I've never been in a store this fucked up in my life.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>OK？我这辈子从没在一家乱成这样的店里呆过。

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:35.090
<v Jason Mascia>Don't get excited. I don't want you to get excited. I want you to just remain calm.
<v Jason Mascia>别激动。我不想让你激动。我只想让你保持冷静。

00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:42.490
<v Sean Cole>This is actually the third time Stephen's made the trek out here from New Jersey. It's a two-hour drive he does not want to make again.
<v Sean Cole>这其实是斯蒂芬第三次从新泽西跑出来。这是他不想再做的两个小时车程。

00:37:42.490 --> 00:37:44.340
<v Stephen Brown>I love the car, but if they don't get--
<v Stephen Brown>我喜欢这辆车，但如果他们不——

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:45.450
<v Stephen Brown>[LAUGHTER]
<v Stephen Brown>[笑声]

00:37:45.450 --> 00:37:46.960
<v Stephen Brown>They're going to have to get it done.
<v Stephen Brown>他们必须把它完成。

00:37:46.960 --> 00:38:07.990
<v Sean Cole>They cannot lose this deal. They're still 10 cars short of their monthly goal. When Stephen does sit down with the finance manager, he can't get the interest rate he wanted, 3.3%. They finally resolve that, but then he doesn't have insurance for the new vehicle, or he hasn't called Geico to transfer the policy from his old car, and he takes care of that, too.
<v Sean Cole>他们不能失去这笔交易。他们距离每月目标还差10辆车。当斯蒂芬坐下来和财务经理谈时，他没能得到他想要的3.3%利率。他们终于解决了那个，但然后他没有新车的保险，或者他还没打过电话给Geico把保单从旧车上转过来，他也处理了那个。

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:27.880
<v Sean Cole>But then, as he's transferring his stuff from the trunk of his old car into the new one, the billing office notices a glitch. Something's wrong with the new insurance card from Geico. Stephen's address isn't on it. And the date on it says October 31, even though it's October 30. That means Stephen won't be able to legally drive the car until the following day.
<v Sean Cole>但然后，当他把东西从旧车的后备箱转到新车里时，结算办公室注意到一个毛病。Geico的新保险卡有点问题。上面没有斯蒂芬的地址。而且上面的日期写着10月31日，尽管今天是10月30日。这意味着斯蒂芬要等到第二天才能合法驾驶这辆车。

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:32.540
<v Jason Mascia>Tiffany, good aft-- good evening. This is Jason. I'm a sales representative at Town & Country Jeep.
<v Jason Mascia>蒂芙尼，下午好——晚上好。我是杰森。我是Town & Country Jeep的销售代表。

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:39.900
<v Sean Cole>Jason gets on the phone with Geico, thinking maybe it'll be an easy fix. It won't be. We all stand around and watch him work this thing.
<v Sean Cole>杰森打给Geico，心想这可能很容易修正。其实不然。我们都站在周围看他处理这件事。

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:44.010
<v Jason Mascia>I'm sitting with a client of yours, and he's purchasing a vehicle, picking it up tonight. I have the ID card.
<v Jason Mascia>我和你的客户坐在一起，他正在买车，今晚提车。我有身份证件。

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:45.920
<v Sean Cole>It's quarter past 10:00 at this point.
<v Sean Cole>此时已经是10点15分。

00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:50.520
<v Jason Mascia>But it's not like he's opening up a new policy. So what are we doing here?
<v Jason Mascia>但这不是像他开启新保单。所以我们在做什么？

00:38:50.520 --> 00:38:52.180
<v Sean Cole>Then it's 10:26.
<v Sean Cole>然后是10点26分。

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:56.320
<v Jason Mascia>Well, we've got to try to make an exception, if you can. Please talk to your supervisor one more time, if you can.
<v Jason Mascia>好吧，我们必须试着破例，如果可以的话。请再和你的主管谈谈，如果可以的话。

00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:58.170
<v Sean Cole>Finally, Tiffany comes through.
<v Sean Cole>最后，蒂芙尼做到了。

00:38:58.170 --> 00:38:59.730
<v Jason Mascia>Oh, my god, you're the best.
<v Jason Mascia>噢，天哪，你是最棒的。

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:07.230
<v Sean Cole>Geico says it'll email a new insurance card. Jason keeps Tiffany, the operator, on the line until everything is squared away with the billing office.
<v Sean Cole>Geico说会电邮一张新的保险卡。杰森让蒂芙尼（操作员）留在在线，直到一切与结算办公室核实清楚。

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:13.370
<v Jason Mascia>How long you been working for Geico? You sound like you really got a lot of enthusiasm for what you do.
<v Jason Mascia>你在Geico工作多久了？你听起来对所做的事情真的很有热情。

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:14.620
<v Tiffany>(LAUGHING) Thank you.
<v Tiffany>（笑着）谢谢。

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:27.940
<v Sean Cole>Billing gives the all clear. We all follow Stephen out to the parking lot. It's about 11 o'clock at this point. Jason giddily watches him tear out of the parking lot and hang a right.
<v Sean Cole>结算办公室放行了。我们都跟着斯蒂芬来到停车场。此时大约是11点。杰森兴高采烈地看着他撕开停车场，向右转。

00:39:27.940 --> 00:39:39.070
<v Jason Mascia>Jersey's that way! He went east. Jersey's west. Oh, my goodness.
<v Jason Mascia>泽西在那边！他往东去了。泽西在西边。噢，天哪。

00:39:39.070 --> 00:39:52.330
<v Ira Glass>Well, coming up, car talk continues. That feels really weird to say. But it's true. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>好了，接下来，汽车谈话继续。说这话感觉真奇怪。但这是真的。那是在从芝加哥公共广播电台接下来的节目继续。

00:39:52.330 --> 00:40:15.010
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program is a rerun. We did all the reporting for today's show a few years ago during a month, October 2013, when car sales were strong, up over 10% what they were a year before around the country. That's 1.2 million new vehicles sold that month. But that doesn't mean that every dealership in every town had an easy time of it.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是重播。我们为今天的节目所做的所有报道都是在几年前，2013年10月，当时汽车销售强劲，比一年前全国增长了10%以上。那是当月售出的120万辆新车。但这并不意味着每个城镇的每家经销店都过得很轻松。

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:35.180
<v Ira Glass>And today on our program, we watch one dealership, just one, on Long Island-- Town & Country Jeep Chrysler Dodge Ram is what it's called-- as they try to make their sales goal of 129 cars for the month. If they sell 129 cars, Chrysler is going to pay them a big bonus, and they need that money to be in the black for the month. If they just sell 128, bupkis.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们看着一家经销店，仅仅一家，在长岛——叫Town & Country Jeep Chrysler Dodge Ram——因为他们试图达成129辆车的月销售目标。如果他们卖掉129辆车，克莱斯勒会支付他们一笔大额奖金，他们需要那笔钱来在该月盈利，而不是亏损。如果只卖128辆，什么都没有。

00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:42.720
<v Ira Glass>So it's 9:15 in the morning on the 31st. How many cars do you got to get?
<v Ira Glass>所以现在是31号早上9点15分。你还得卖多少辆车？

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:43.930
<v Freddie Hoyt>Nine.
<v Freddie Hoyt>9辆。

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:46.580
<v Ira Glass>That's Freddie, the general manager here.
<v Ira Glass>那是弗雷迪，这里的总经理。

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:49.080
<v Freddie Hoyt>Nine more.
<v Freddie Hoyt>再卖9辆。

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:52.050
<v Ira Glass>And what do you have to say about that?
<v Ira Glass>对此你有什么要说的吗？

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.960
<v Freddie Hoyt>God help us.
<v Freddie Hoyt>上帝保佑我们。

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:55.340
<v Freddie Hoyt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Freddie Hoyt>[笑声]

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:58.440
<v Freddie Hoyt>God help us. I don't know. It's going to be-- it's going to be hard.
<v Freddie Hoyt>上帝保佑我们。我不知道。这会是——这会很难。

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:11.280
<v Ira Glass>On a good month, he would start the last day with just two or three cars to sell. Nine. That's crazy. And it's Halloween, which means that lots of potential customers are going to be out with their kids today.
<v Ira Glass>在好的月份，他会在最后一天开始时只需要卖两三辆车。9辆。这太疯狂了。而且今天是万圣节，这意味着今天会有很多潜在客户和孩子们在一起。

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:16.870
<v Ira Glass>Everybody is so tense that it's not even 10:00 in the morning and a customer walks out without buying a car.
<v Ira Glass>每个人都太紧张了，甚至还不到早上10点，一个客户没买车就走了。

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:20.930
<v Customer>No, we're done. Sorry, but we're done. I came in to make a deal, and that's not what I was expecting.
<v Customer>不，我们完了。抱歉，但我们完了。我进来是为了达成交易，但这并不是我所期待的。

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:22.050
<v Manny Rosales>Thank you. Have a good night.
<v Manny Rosales>谢谢。晚安。

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:27.910
<v Ira Glass>And one of the managers, Sal, has this edgy little conversation with the salesman who let him walk out.
<v Ira Glass>其中一位经理萨尔，和那个让他走掉的销售员进行了这次紧张的小对话。

00:41:27.910 --> 00:41:30.610
<v Sal Lanzilotta>What happened to that guy you were working?
<v Sal Lanzilotta>那个你正在处理的家伙怎么了？

00:41:30.610 --> 00:41:31.350
<v Manny Rosales>He left.
<v Manny Rosales>他走了。

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:34.530
<v Sal Lanzilotta>You've got to stop doing that, bro.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>你必须停止那样做，兄弟。

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.720
<v Manny Rosales>How do I stop doing that? You don't help. You got a meeting--
<v Manny Rosales>我怎么停止那样做？你没帮忙。你有一个会议——

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:42.010
<v Sal Lanzilotta>You didn't even come up here. How am I supposed to help? Where's my crystal ball, in the shop? Do it again, you're following him.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>你甚至没上来。我怎么帮你？我的水晶球在哪，在店里吗？再做一次，你跟着他走。

00:41:42.010 --> 00:41:44.400
<v Manny Rosales>Let's do it. Let's do it right now.
<v Manny Rosales>我们做吧。我们现在就做。

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:46.020
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Go ahead. Pack up. Go.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>继续。收拾行李。走。

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:48.080
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't go.
<v Ira Glass>他没走。

00:41:48.080 --> 00:41:49.750
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Un-fucking-real.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>真他妈的不真实。

00:41:49.750 --> 00:42:04.700
<v Ira Glass>The morning's still young when I find Freddie in his office selling a car to himself-- a loaner car for the maintenance department to lend out to customers who are getting their cars repaired. He's allowed to count that in his sales total.
<v Ira Glass>早上还早的时候，我发现弗雷迪在办公室里卖车给自己——一辆给维护部门借给正在维修车辆的客户的备用车。他被允许将此计入他的销售总额中。

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:08.530
<v Freddie Hoyt>So now we only need eight more. See, it's starting to come together already.
<v Freddie Hoyt>所以现在我们只需要再卖8辆。你看，它已经开始成形了。

00:42:08.530 --> 00:42:26.250
<v Ira Glass>By mid-afternoon, he'll buy a second loaner car for the dealership. Back in February, Freddie bought a Durango for his wife to make the number that month, replacing a car that he had just bought a year before for her for the same reason.
<v Ira Glass>到下午，他会为经销店买第二辆备用车。早在二月，弗雷迪为妻子买了一辆Durango以达到那个月的数字，更换了他一年前为了同样原因刚买给她的车。

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:33.760
<v Freddie Hoyt>Joe? What's the story with your cars?
<v Freddie Hoyt>乔？你的车是怎么回事？

00:42:33.760 --> 00:42:46.300
<v Ira Glass>Freddie is calling Joe Monti, one of the guys who runs the used car side of the business at Town & Country. Joe has got his own goal to hit for the month that is separate from the new car goal. 70 used cars. That's the number Freddie set for him.
<v Ira Glass>弗雷迪在给乔·蒙蒂打电话，负责Town & Country二手车业务的家伙之一。乔有自己的月度目标要达到，与新车目标分开。70辆二手车。那是弗雷迪给他设定的数字。

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:48.520
<v Freddie Hoyt>Is any of them going out yet or what? What are we doing?
<v Freddie Hoyt>它们出去了一辆吗还是怎样？我们在做什么？

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:49.650
<v Joe Monti>They're all going out, boss.
<v Joe Monti>它们都出去了，老板。

00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:52.630
<v Freddie Hoyt>OK, but how come none was even delivered yet? I'm still sitting at six.
<v Freddie Hoyt>好的，但为什么一辆都没交付？我还坐在6辆上。

00:42:52.630 --> 00:42:54.580
<v Joe Monti>Yuri's delivered the Chevy Trailblazer.
<v Joe Monti>尤里交付了雪佛兰开拓者。

00:42:54.580 --> 00:42:55.690
<v Freddie Hoyt>Hold on. What?
<v Freddie Hoyt>等等。什么？

00:42:55.690 --> 00:43:08.880
<v Ira Glass>While we were at Town & Country, we asked everybody what it would mean for him or her personally if the team did not make its sales goal. And it was actually this guy, Joe Monti, over in used cars, who gave us the best picture of that. Sarah Koenig hung out with him for a while.
<v Ira Glass>在Town & Country时，我们问每个人，如果团队没达成销售目标，对他个人意味着什么。事实上是这个家伙，二手车的乔·蒙蒂，给了我们最好的写照。莎拉·柯尼格和他呆了一段时间。

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:15.980
<v Sarah Koenig>Joe's particularly stressed out this month. Half the time I'm with him, it feels like he's fighting, even with customers.
<v Sarah Koenig>乔这个月特别紧张。我陪他的一半时间里，感觉他都在抗争，甚至是对抗客户。

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:26.910
<v Joe Monti>Nobody's giving you a runaround, Jessica, OK? Yelling and screaming and being rude and threatening is not going to get you anywhere. The car-- the car-- the car is a great car.
<v Joe Monti>没人给你设圈套，杰西卡，OK？大喊大叫、粗鲁无礼和威胁是不会有用的。这辆车——这辆车——这辆车是一辆好车。

00:43:26.910 --> 00:43:32.360
<v Sarah Koenig>Next fight, he calls a woman in the back office who he thinks ratted to Freddie about a deal she didn't like.
<v Sarah Koenig>下一次争吵，他给后台的一位女士打电话，他认为她向弗雷迪举报了一笔她不喜欢的交易。

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:55.900
<v Joe Monti>Theresa? Well, can you pick up the phone? Nails could get done later. Why did you run-- why did you run to Freddie? Why did you run to Freddie and tell Freddie about a Chrysler 300? Whose conversation? Why were you talking to Eddie? First of all, if a manager writes off on a deal and signs it-- she hung up the phone.
<v Joe Monti>特蕾莎？好吧，你能接电话吗？指甲以后再弄。你为什么跑——你为什么跑去弗雷迪那里？你为什么跑去弗雷迪那里，告诉弗雷迪关于一辆克莱斯勒300的事？谁的谈话？你为什么和埃迪谈？首先，如果经理批准了一笔交易并签了字——她挂了电话。

00:43:55.900 --> 00:44:07.910
<v Sarah Koenig>I'm sort of surprised anyone would have the balls to fight with Joe. He's a big man-- 350 pounds. Large and in charge. The day I meet him, he's wearing a gray tracksuit,
<v Sarah Koenig>我有点惊讶竟然有人有胆量和乔吵架。他是个大块头——350磅。庞大且负责。我见他的那天，他穿着灰色运动服，

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:21.260
<v Sarah Koenig>but what I quickly find out is that he's wearing a tracksuit because it's his day off. He's supposed to be at his son Mike's football game against Malvern. He hasn't been able to catch an away game all season, and he took this one Sunday off to do it. It was important to him.
<v Sarah Koenig>但我很快发现他穿运动服是因为那是他的休息日。他本应去参加他儿子迈克对抗马尔文的足球比赛。整个赛季他都没能赶上一场客场比赛，而他请了这个周日休息去参加。这对他很重要。

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:31.480
<v Sarah Koenig>For so many years, he wasn't around enough for his kids. He's working all the time. Joe knows Mike will be looking for him in the stands right about now. And he's not going to be there because he's here at the car lot.
<v Sarah Koenig>多年来，他陪伴孩子的时间不够。他总是整天工作。乔知道迈克现在就在看台上找他。而他不会在那里，因为他在汽车地段。

00:44:31.480 --> 00:44:43.220
<v Joe Monti>We're a little shorthanded, so I've got to be here, which is not a problem. Listen, if I'm making money, I'm making money. Just it's a catch-22. You're missing a game, but that's the car business.
<v Joe Monti>我们人手有点不足，所以我必须在这里，这不是问题。听着，如果我在赚钱，我就是在赚钱。只是这是一个两难境地。你错过了一场比赛，但这就是汽车生意。

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:44.980
<v Joe Monti>[LAUGHTER]
<v Joe Monti>[笑声]

00:44:44.980 --> 00:44:48.080
<v Joe Monti>That's the car business. I'll make it up to him later.
<v Joe Monti>这就是汽车生意。我以后会补偿他的。

00:44:48.080 --> 00:45:03.360
<v Sarah Koenig>Yesterday, Joe was late to his mother-in-law's surprise 70th birthday party. And last week, he was late for his own mother's 60th. The used car side of Town & Country is supposed to sell 70 cars by the end of the month. That's Joe's version of the 129 on the new side.
<v Sarah Koenig>昨天，乔去参加他岳母的70岁惊喜生日派对迟到了。上周，他参加他自己母亲的60岁生日也迟到了。Town & Country的二手车方被要求在月底前卖出70辆车。那是乔对新车方129辆的那个版本。

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:15.290
<v Sarah Koenig>When I first met Joe, used was at 35. At stake is Joe's big year-end bonus. Joe wouldn't tell me the amount of the bonus, but he made it clear it's very, very nice. Down-payment-on-a-house nice.
<v Sarah Koenig>当我第一次见到乔时，二手车是35辆。赌注是乔的大额年终奖金。乔没告诉我奖金数额，但他明确表示这非常、非常好。能在房子上付首付的那种好。

00:45:15.290 --> 00:45:32.170
<v Sarah Koenig>Since he fell so behind the last couple months, this month, October, is make or break for Joe. He's got to get to at least 70 cars if he wants any hope of reaching that bonus by the end of the year. And it's not looking good. 20 years in the business, Joe's never seen a stretch this slow for this long.
<v Sarah Koenig>因为过去几个月他落后太多，这个月，十月，对乔来说是成败在此一举。如果他想在年底前有任何希望获得那笔奖金，他必须达到至少70辆车。而且情况看起来不好。在这个行业干了20年，乔从未见过这么慢、这么久的一段时期。

00:45:32.170 --> 00:45:46.520
<v Sarah Koenig>Joe blames it mostly on Hurricane Sandy. A year ago, tens of thousands of people lost their cars all at once. And they got new ones all at once. So now, a year later, tens of thousands of people are out of the used car market who normally would be buying and selling right now.
<v Sarah Koenig>乔主要把这归咎于飓风桑迪。一年前，成千上万的人同时失去了他们的车。他们同时得到了新车。所以现在，一年后，成千上万的人退出了二手车市场，而他们本该在此时买卖的。

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:48.900
<v Joe Monti>We had two weekends in a row we sold one car.
<v Joe Monti>我们连续两个周末只卖了一辆车。

00:45:48.900 --> 00:45:50.020
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, my god.
<v Sarah Koenig>噢，天哪。

00:45:50.020 --> 00:45:53.980
<v Joe Monti>For two Saturdays and two Sundays, and we sold one car.
<v Joe Monti>两个周六和两个周日，我们只卖了一辆车。

00:45:53.980 --> 00:45:55.370
<v Sarah Koenig>In the whole weekend?
<v Sarah Koenig>整个周末？

00:45:55.370 --> 00:46:01.200
<v Joe Monti>The whole weekend. The whole two weekends, we sold one car. There was days we didn't see a customer.
<v Joe Monti>整个周末。那两个完整的周末，我们卖了一辆车。甚至有几天我们没见到一个客户。

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:19.400
<v Sarah      Koenig>Joe goes outside to do an appraisal on a Nissan Quest. This also gives him a chance to have a cigarette, one of about 40 Parliaments he'll smoke today. His wife and kids get on him about the smoking. He's got six kids. Six kids-- two are grown, four of them still at home.
<v Sarah      Koenig>乔出去评估一辆日产Quest。这也让他有机会抽支烟，他今天会抽的约40支Parliament之一。他的妻子和孩子们因为吸烟而纠缠他。他有六个孩子。六个孩子——两个长大了，四个还在家里。

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:32.970
<v Sarah      Koenig>Joe's the only breadwinner in the family-- always has been-- so it's all on him. Even in the best of times, Joe's a worrier, like his grandmother. He's 42 years old, but he looks older. In the past year, he's gained about 80 pounds.
<v Sarah      Koenig>乔是家里唯一的经济支柱——一直都是——所以压力全在他身上。即使在最好的时候，乔也是个忧虑者，像他祖母一样。他42岁，但看起来更老。在过去的一年里，他胖了约80磅。

00:46:32.970 --> 00:46:49.010
<v Sarah      Koenig>In the parking lot, Joe throws away a half-smoked cigarette and then looks over the Nissan Quest. Tires are so-so. Needs some detailing. It's got a cracked taillight and a bad CARFAX report, meaning it's been in an accident. $2,000 is fair for this car, Joe says. It's what these are going for at auction.
<v Sarah      Koenig>在停车场，乔扔掉了一支抽了一半的烟，然后检查那辆日产Quest。轮胎一般。需要做些细节处理。它有一个裂开的尾灯和一份糟糕的CARFAX报告，意味着它发生过事故。这辆车2,000美元很公道，乔说。这是它们在拍卖会上的价格。

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:56.450
<v Sarah      Koenig>He tells me the customers are going to want at least $2,500, but there's no way he can do that. $2,500 is just too much. He heads back inside.
<v Sarah      Koenig>他告诉我客户至少会想要2,500美元，但他绝对做不到。2,500美元太多了。他走回里面。

00:46:56.450 --> 00:46:58.490
<v Joe Monti>$2,500, that's everything.
<v Joe Monti>2,500美元，那可是全部了。

00:46:58.490 --> 00:47:00.040
<v Sarah Koenig>Instantly caves.
<v Sarah Koenig>瞬间屈服。

00:47:00.040 --> 00:47:01.150
<v Joe Monti>That's what I will do.
<v Joe Monti>这就是我会做的。

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:05.310
<v Sarah Koenig>He needs the deal. They shake on it.
<v Sarah Koenig>他需要这笔交易。他们握手成交。

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:15.580
<v Sarah Koenig>Joe was about 22 when he started in the car business. He had a baby at 18-- his son, Joseph-- and then four more kids-- Dean, Chris, Michael, and Adriana, plus his stepson, Louis.
<v Sarah Koenig>乔大约22岁时开始进入汽车行业。他在18岁时就有了孩子——他的儿子，约瑟夫——然后又是四个孩子——迪恩、克里斯、迈克尔和阿德里安娜，加上他的继子路易斯。

00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:37.850
<v Sarah Koenig>Joe himself was one of five kids. His mom raised them by herself, no dad around, no money. He moved around a lot. With his own kids, he's the uptight parent, the one who doesn't laugh enough, the one who hammers them about consequences. He wants his kids to fear his wrath but also to know he loves them. Sometimes, it's tricky for him to balance that or even to talk about it.
<v Sarah Koenig>乔自己是五个孩子之一。他妈妈独自抚养他们，没有父亲在场，没钱。他搬了很多次家。对他自己的孩子，他是那个紧张的家长，那个笑得不够多、那个猛烈抨击他们后果的家长。他希望他的孩子敬畏他的愤怒，但也知道他爱他们。有时，对他来说平衡这一点，甚至谈论它都很棘手。

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:54.480
<v Joe Monti>I have a good relationship with my children. I didn't have a father. So it's hard to do something when you don't know how to do it. Sometimes you don't know how to do-- I'm like a manly man.
<v Joe Monti>我和我的孩子们关系很好。我没有父亲。所以当你不知道怎么做时，做某事很难。有时你不知道该怎么做——我像一个硬汉。

00:47:54.480 --> 00:48:07.000
<v Joe Monti>So I love them very much, but I don't know how to sometimes show it to them. I kiss them. I hug them. But you know what I'm saying? Something that I have to adapt to. But that's it. They're good. They're good kids.
<v Joe Monti>所以我非常爱他们，但我有时不知道怎么向他们展示。我亲吻他们。我拥抱他们。但你知道我在说什么吗？这是我必须适应的事情。但就这样。他们很好。他们是好孩子。

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:32.600
<v Sarah Koenig>Joe wants his kids to have more stability than he had growing up and a better standard of living. For 16 years, they all lived in a big house in Smithtown in a fairly wealthy neighborhood. And Joe says there was pressure there. Some other kids at school had fancy clothes and extravagant vacations, blow-out sweet 16 parties. He didn't want his kids to feel less-than, so he made sure, within reason, that they had what the other kids had.
<v Sarah Koenig>乔希望他的孩子拥有比他成长时期更多的稳定性，更好的生活水平。16年来，他们都住在史密斯镇一个相当富裕的社区的一栋大房子里。乔说那里有压力。学校里有些其他孩子有漂亮的衣服和奢侈的假期，挥霍的16岁生日派对。他不想让他的孩子感到不如人，所以他确保在合理范围内，他们拥有其他孩子所拥有的一切。

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:55.070
<v Sarah Koenig>But three years ago, he left Town & Country for another job at a Toyota dealer, and it didn't go well at all. He lost-- well, he says he lost everything. But in any case, it was a lot of money. They sold their big house in Smithtown and moved to a smaller house in Levittown, about five minutes from the dealership. And in a lot of ways, Joe's glad about that. It's made them closer as a family, he says.
<v Sarah Koenig>但三年前，他为了另一份工作离开Town & Country去了丰田经销商，结果完全不顺。他失去了——好吧，他说他失去了一切。但无论如何，那是很多钱。他们卖掉了史密斯镇的大房子，搬到了莱维敦的一栋小房子里，距离经销店约五分钟路程。而在很多方面，乔对此很高兴。他说这让他们作为一个家庭更亲密了。

00:48:55.070 --> 00:49:07.350
<v Sarah Koenig>But he's also trying to work his way back up. He'd like to be able to buy them all a bigger house, give everyone a little more room. That's why he's so worried about this bad stretch of business and about getting that bonus.
<v Sarah Koenig>但他也在努力往上爬。他希望能给他们买一栋更大的房子，给每个人多一点空间。这就是为什么他如此担心这段糟糕的生意，以及那笔奖金。

00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:13.000
<v Sarah Koenig>Toward the end of the day, his son Mike calls. Joe's already spoken to the coach about how the football game went.
<v Sarah Koenig>接近一天结束时，他儿子迈克打来电话。乔已经和教练谈过足球比赛的情况了。

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:15.540
<v Joe Monti>Two sacks and two tackles. That's great.
<v Joe Monti>两次擒杀和两次扑击。那太棒了。

00:49:15.540 --> 00:49:17.710
<v Mike Monti>OK. Wait, Dad.
<v Mike Monti>OK。等等，爸爸。

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:20.040
<v Joe Monti>What?
<v Joe Monti>什么？

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:25.180
<v Mike Monti>Do you think [INAUDIBLE] to your job so you can give us some money so we can go get something to eat?
<v Mike Monti>你觉得[听不清]到你的工作，这样你就可以给我们一些钱，我们可以去买点吃的吗？

00:49:25.180 --> 00:49:32.680
<v Joe Monti>Yeah. All right. I'll be home in probably, hopefully, like 20 minutes, and I'll give you money to go out to eat.
<v Joe Monti>是的。好的。我大约——希望如此——20分钟后回家，我会给你钱出去吃饭。

00:49:32.680 --> 00:49:34.330
<v Mike Monti>OK. [INAUDIBLE].
<v Mike Monti>OK。[听不清]。

00:49:34.330 --> 00:49:36.500
<v Joe Monti>All right. Bye.
<v Joe Monti>好的。拜。

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:53.430
<v Joe Monti>Every one of them. And it's hard to say no. You try your best. When you're doing well, it's no big deal, and you can take care of them. And then there's just doing well to where you're making it by.
<v Joe Monti>每一个都是这样。很难说不。你尽你所能。当你做得好的时候，没大不了，你可以照顾他们。然后就是做得刚好维持生计的时候。

00:49:53.430 --> 00:50:09.770
<v Joe Monti>What is it? Today's October. What's going to happen in a couple of months when my son's get ready to leave for college? Am I going to have the money?
<v Joe Monti>这是什么？今天是十月。几个月后我儿子准备去上大学时会发生什么？我会有钱吗？

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:32.530
<v Ira Glass>It's mid-afternoon on the new car side of Town & Country. So what do you do all day on the last day of the month when you need to sell everything you possibly can? Well, instead of waiting for customers to come into the store, you call up any lead you have and try to close deals right over the phone if you can.
<v Ira Glass>那是Town & Country新车方下午。那么在月底最后一天，当你需要尽可能多地卖掉所有东西时，你整天做什么？好吧，与其等待客户走进店里，你打给任何你有的潜在客户，如果可以，就在电话里谈成交易。

00:50:32.530 --> 00:50:49.920
<v Ira Glass>One of the salespeople, Lori, does this with a previous customer. Jason also sells to a guy he knows over the phone. Peter gets a lead from a cop upstate that he sold a Durango truck to. The lead is a friend of the cop's, also a cop, who wants the same deal his friend got. Peter gets him on the phone and says he'll give him the deal for two reasons.
<v Ira Glass>其中一位销售员，洛丽，和一个之前的客户这么做了。杰森也通过电话卖给了一个他认识的家伙。彼得从一位住在外地的警察那里得到了一个潜在客户，他曾卖给那警察一辆Durango卡车。那个潜在客户是那警察的朋友，也是位警察，想要和他朋友一样的交易。彼得在电话里和他谈，说他会给他那个交易，有两个原因。

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:57.460
<v Peter Possas>One of them, you were referred. And the other one is you're an officer of the law, and we respect that over here, big time, just so you know.
<v Peter Possas>其中一个，你被推荐了。另一个是你是一位法律官员，我们在这里非常尊重这一点，只是让你知道。

00:50:57.460 --> 00:51:15.730
<v Ira Glass>Of course, this leaves out the third and main reason for the great deal that he's getting-- they need eight more sales by 9 o'clock. And the cop ends up buying his Durango for $2,214 less than the dealership paid for it. Even after finance charges and holdback and all the other ways they make money, they still lost on this one.
<v Ira Glass>当然，这忽略了他得到的那个伟大交易的第三个也是主要原因——他们在9点前还需要8辆销售额。而那位警察最终买下的Durango比经销店付出的价格少了2,214美元。即使在财务费用、留存和他们赚钱的其他方式之后，他们在这个项目上仍然亏损。

00:51:15.730 --> 00:51:31.490
<v Ira Glass>Ditto this guy, Richard Massana. He was going to get an oil change this morning and stepped in on a whim and they showed him numbers way lower than any other dealer that he'd been to. Richard called up his wife to let her know, hey, surprise, we're getting a new truck. On his test drive, he still seemed amazed at what was happening.
<v Ira Glass>理查德·马萨纳这家伙也是如此。他本来早上要换油，一时兴起进来了，他们向他展示了比他去过的任何其他经销商都低得多的数字。理查德打电话给他的妻子，让她知道，嘿，惊喜，我们要买一辆新卡车。在试驾时，他似乎对他所经历的一切感到惊讶。

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:38.350
<v Richard Massana>Who would have thought Halloween, buying a truck? I never thought it would happen.
<v Richard Massana>谁会想到万圣节买卡车？我从没想过会发生。

00:51:38.350 --> 00:51:54.510
<v Ira Glass>His payment will be $425 a month. The salesman says later that it should have been $525, but it's the end of the month. Turns out, yes, it is true that you do get a better deal at the end of the month at Town & Country-- at least any month that they're struggling to make their goal.
<v Ira Glass>他的月供是425美元。销售员后来表示本来应该是525美元，但这是月底。结果证明，是的，确实没错，在Town & Country月底确实能得到更好的交易——至少在他们努力达成目标的任何月份。

00:51:54.510 --> 00:52:17.760
<v Ira Glass>When you actually look at the numbers, the cars that they sell on the last two days, the average deal actually loses money versus the deals that they make at the beginning of the month, where they made about $900 per car. OK, for car people, I'm now going to say these words. I am factoring in everything they made on financing and warranties and extras and holdback and the 1% they get from Chrysler on every car when I make that statement.
<v Ira Glass>当你真正查看数字时，他们在最后两天卖出的车，平均交易实际上是在亏钱，对比他们在月初做的交易（每辆车大约赚900美元）。好的，对于汽车人，我现在要说这些话。我在做那个陈述时，把他们在融资、保修、额外项目上赚的所有钱，以及克莱斯勒给的1%留存都计算在内了。

00:52:17.760 --> 00:52:40.610
<v Ira Glass>I am not factoring in any money that they would get if they hit their number and get their bonus from Chrysler. That would add about $500 per car, which still does not give them a huge profit per car. It used to be a lot higher. But today, because people research prices on the internet, profit margins on new cars are lower than what they used to be, not just here at this dealership, but everywhere in the car business.
<v Ira Glass>我没有把如果他们达到数字并从克莱斯勒获得奖金时他们可能得到的任何钱计算在内。那会给每辆车增加大约500美元，这仍然不能给他们每辆车带来巨大的利润。它曾经高得多。但今天，因为人们在互联网上研究价格，新车的利润率比以前低了，不仅是在这家店，在整个汽车行业都是如此。

00:52:40.610 --> 00:52:54.560
<v Ira Glass>The owner of Town & Country told me that he used to make $500 more per car, and each salesman made more per car. Now they all have to make it up in volume. So it's more of a grind, a harder job with tighter deals and less reward.
<v Ira Glass>Town & Country的老板告诉我，他每辆车曾经多赚500美元，每个销售员每辆车也赚得更多。现在他们必须通过成交量来弥补。所以这更像是一种苦差事，一份利润更薄、交易更紧、回报更少的工作。

00:52:54.560 --> 00:53:19.490
<v Ira Glass>And if you're wondering, car dealerships make less profit than other businesses. A recent study by an outfit called Sageworks found that car dealers end up with less than $0.03 on every dollar they make as profit, which is less profit than furniture stores, office supply stores, restaurants. Lower than pretty much everything but grocery stores. When car dealers say that they are not making a killing, on average, it's true.
<v Ira Glass>如果你感到好奇，汽车经销店的利润比其他企业少。Sageworks最近的一项研究发现，汽车经销商每赚1美元作为利润的不到0.03美元，这比家具店、办公用品店、餐馆的利润都少。比几乎所有东西都低，除了杂货店。当汽车经销商说他们平均没有在大捞一笔时，这是真的。

00:53:19.490 --> 00:53:52.200
<v Ira Glass>At some point during this last day of the month, one of the deals that they had been counting on falls through. Mike Lester's sale of a red Grand Cherokee. But turns out that's no problem for the dealership because Scott has a customer who wants that exact same truck. You remember that deal the day before, where two salesmen sold the same car to two different customers. The mortician? It's that salesman and that customer, and the salesman, Scott, is very, very happy. And the only bad news about all of this is what it does to Mike Lester's sales total for the month.
<v Ira Glass>在本月最后一天期间的某个时刻，他们一直指望的一笔交易搞砸了。迈克·莱斯特卖出一辆红色大切诺基。但结果这对经销店来说没问题，因为斯科蒂有一个客户想要那辆同样的卡车。你记得前一天的那个交易吗，两个销售员把同一辆车卖给了两个不同的客户。那个殡葬承办人？就是那个销售员和那个客户，而销售员斯科蒂，非常、非常高兴。而所有这一切的唯一坏消息是它对迈克·莱斯特当月销售总额的影响。

00:53:52.200 --> 00:53:54.520
<v Mike Lester>That drops me down to 14.
<v Mike Lester>这把我降到了14。

00:53:54.520 --> 00:54:04.880
<v Ira Glass>14 sucks because, like I said earlier, 15 is the magic number. 15 is where the serious bonuses and commissions kick in. Most of the guys count on getting those.
<v Ira Glass>14很糟糕，因为，正如我之前所说，15是神奇数字。15是严重奖金和佣金生效的地方。大多数家伙指望获得那些。

00:54:04.880 --> 00:54:07.730
<v Mike Lester>It could add up to big money.
<v Mike Lester>它可能加起来是一大笔钱。

00:54:07.730 --> 00:54:15.400
<v Ira Glass>But then, Scott, whose customer is now going to get the red Grand Cherokee, Scott realizes what this means for Mike and says to Mike--
<v Ira Glass>但然后，斯科蒂，那位客户现在要得到红色大切诺基的家伙，斯科蒂意识到这对迈克意味着什么，并对迈克说——

00:54:15.400 --> 00:54:16.600
<v Scott Froehlich>Wait, is that your 15th car?
<v Scott Froehlich>等等，那是你的第15辆车吗？

00:54:16.600 --> 00:54:17.600
<v Mike Lester>Yeah.
<v Mike Lester>是的。

00:54:17.600 --> 00:54:19.620
<v Scott Froehlich>You take the chip. Give him the chip. Switch it around.
<v Scott Froehlich>你拿走筹码。给他筹码。切换一下。

00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:21.250
<v Mike Lester>OK.
<v Mike Lester>好的。

00:54:21.250 --> 00:54:22.660
<v Scott Froehlich>And split it with me.
<v Scott Froehlich>和我也分摊一下。

00:54:22.660 --> 00:54:23.750
<v Mike Lester>OK.
<v Mike Lester>好的。

00:54:23.750 --> 00:54:27.960
<v Ira Glass>Now Mike will get the credit for the sale. He's at 15 again.
<v Ira Glass>现在迈克会获得销售信用。他回到15了。

00:54:27.960 --> 00:54:30.780
<v Mike Lester>OK, yeah. Love you, Scott.
<v Mike Lester>好的，是的。爱你，斯科蒂。

00:54:30.780 --> 00:54:34.860
<v Ira Glass>Scott kisses Mike on the cheek. Producer Brian Reed witnesses all this.
<v Ira Glass>斯科蒂亲了迈克的脸颊。制作人布莱恩·里德目睹了这一切。

00:54:34.860 --> 00:54:35.900
<v Brian Reed>So you're at 15 now?
<v Brian Reed>所以你现在是15了？

00:54:35.900 --> 00:54:37.310
<v Mike Lester>Yes.
<v Mike Lester>是的。

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:38.730
<v Brian Reed>Did he owe you one, or?
<v Brian Reed>他欠你一个人情，还是？

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:43.550
<v Mike Lester>No, but we always help each other out, especially on the last day of the month.
<v Mike Lester>不，但我们总是互相帮助，尤其是在月底最后一天。

00:54:43.550 --> 00:54:46.510
<v Brian Reed>How do you feel?
<v Brian Reed>你感觉怎么样？

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:48.970
<v Mike Lester>Excited.
<v Mike Lester>兴奋。

00:54:48.970 --> 00:54:49.770
<v Mike Lester>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Lester>[笑声]

00:54:49.770 --> 00:55:13.940
<v Ira Glass>So amidst anxious salesmen and salesmen literally running to the desk or to finance, there is one salesman who is a model of calm. Even his cubicle is different from everybody else's. There's no swag. There's no pictures of family. There are no good luck charms. It's bare. The salesman, his name is Manny Rosales, says he doesn't like the distractions. One of our producers, Brian, who you just heard, got to know him.
<v Ira Glass>所以在一群焦虑的销售员中，在这些甚至 literalmente 奔向服务台或财务部的销售员中，有一位销售员是冷静的典范。甚至他的工位也和别人不一样。没有赠品。没有家庭照片。没有好运符。它是空的。这位销售员，他的名字是曼尼·罗萨莱斯，说他不喜欢分心。我们的制作人之一布莱恩（你刚才听到的那个）和他认识了。

00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:27.710
<v Brian Reed>Manny is 58, born in Peru. He has gray hair and glasses. One morning at the showroom, he gestured cryptically for me to come over to his desk, as if he had a secret to tell me. Turn that off, he said, pointing to my tape recorder.
<v Brian Reed>曼尼58岁，出生于秘鲁。他有灰白的头发和眼镜。一天早上在展厅，他神秘地示意我到他的办公桌前，好像他有一个秘密要告诉我。关掉那个，他说，指着我的录音机。

00:55:27.710 --> 00:55:44.610
<v Brian Reed>I explained that I was a radio reporter and I needed to record whatever he was going to tell me for it to be useful, but he wasn't having it. He seemed unimpressed by our little radio story. He said my colleagues and I were like fishermen who had no idea what we were angling for. We had no focus. We were just casting blindly.
<v Brian Reed>我解释说我是一名广播记者，我需要记录下他要说的任何东西才能有用，但他不吃这一套。他似乎对我们的小广播故事没印象。他说我和我的同事就像那些根本不知道自己在钓什么的渔夫。我们没有焦点。我们只是盲目地投下钓线。

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:59.130
<v Brian Reed>Manny, I soon learned, is a man of many metaphors. I put down my recorder. The thing you need to be paying attention to, he said, is the negotiation. The salesman is the tiger, and the customer is the deer.
<v Brian Reed>曼尼，我很快了解到，是一个多隐喻的人。我放下了录音机。你需要注意的事情，他说，是谈判。销售员是老虎，客户是鹿。

00:55:59.130 --> 00:56:17.930
<v Brian Reed>The tiger has to eat, and you can't eat if you don't kill that deer. He moved his right hand up and then clamped his fingers around his own neck. You have to go for the neck, he said. But if you try to kill him too early, the deer will wake up and run away.
<v Brian Reed>老虎必须吃东西，如果你不杀死那只鹿，你就没法吃。他把右手向上移动，然后把手指夹在自己的脖子上。你必须冲着脖子去，他说。但如果你过早尝试杀死他，鹿会醒来并跑掉。

00:56:17.930 --> 00:56:45.570
<v Brian Reed>As the day went on, I kept trying to get Manny to let me record him, but he wouldn't. Instead, he'd come up to me while I was interviewing someone else, point to his head very deliberately, presumably to his brain, give me a knowing look, and then walk away. At one point, we found ourselves facing each other at his desk. He asked me if I had read the greatest book ever written about car sales. I don't think so, I told him. What is it? The Art of War, he said, by Sun Tzu.
<v Brian Reed>随着一天的推移，我一直试图让曼尼让我录音，但他不肯。相反，当我采访别人时，他会走到我面前，非常刻意地指着他的头，大概是他的大脑，给我一个心领神会的眼神，然后走开。有一次，我们发现彼此面对面坐在他的办公桌前。他问我是否读过有史以来写过的关于汽车销售的最伟大的书。我想没有，我告诉他。是什么？《孙子兵法》，他说，孙武。

00:56:45.570 --> 00:57:09.290
<v Brian Reed>Sun Tzu, as you maybe know, was a military general and strategist in ancient China. The Art of War is his magnum opus, where he lays out his tactics for how to succeed in conflict. And though Sun Tzu was writing about actual war, the book has become well known in business and sales circles. It's kind of like The Seven Habits of Highly Effective People, if that had been written in the fifth century BC on individual strips of bamboo.
<v Brian Reed>孙武，正如你可能知道的，是中国古代的一位军事将军和战略家。《孙子兵法》是他的巅峰之作，他在其中阐述了他如何在冲突中取得成功的战术。虽然孙武写的是实际战争，但这本书在商业和销售圈子里变得众所周知。它有点像《高效能人士的七个习惯》，如果那是公元前五世纪在单独的竹条上写的。

00:57:09.290 --> 00:57:12.420
<v Brian Reed>So I went to the bookstore and bought a copy and then headed back to Town & Country.
<v Brian Reed>所以我去了书店买了一本，然后回到Town & Country。

00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:17.180
<v Brian Reed>Manny, how are you, sir? Can I show you the homework I've been doing?
<v Brian Reed>曼尼，你好，先生。我可以给你看看我一直在做的家庭作业吗？

00:57:17.180 --> 00:57:18.020
<v Manny Rosales>Please.
<v Manny Rosales>请。

00:57:18.020 --> 00:57:32.210
<v Brian Reed>I handed him my copy of The Art of War, which I'd marked up with notes about its relevance to car sales. I could see why Manny felt that it spoke to his profession. Lines like, "draw them in with the prospect of gain," "take them by confusion" seemed especially on point.
<v Brian Reed>我把我的《孙子兵法》副本交给他，我已经标记了关于它与汽车销售相关性的笔记。我能看出为什么曼尼觉得它说到了他的职业。像“以利诱之”，“以混乱制胜”这样的句子似乎特别到位。

00:57:32.210 --> 00:57:33.180
<v Manny Rosales>How far are you?
<v Manny Rosales>你读到哪了？

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:35.600
<v Brian Reed>I finished it.
<v Brian Reed>读完了。

00:57:35.600 --> 00:57:39.870
<v Manny Rosales>You finished it? OK. I want to ask you a question. What have you learned?
<v Manny Rosales>你读完了？好的。我想问你一个问题。你学到了什么？

00:57:39.870 --> 00:57:45.230
<v Brian Reed>What have I learned? Well, I'm here to learn some more.
<v Brian Reed>我学到了什么？好吧，我来这里是为了学到更多。

00:57:45.230 --> 00:57:53.600
<v Ira Glass>And I was in. For two days, Manny took me under his wing with my recorder running and became my sales sensei.
<v Ira Glass>我进去了。两天里，曼尼把我带在身边，我的录音机开着，成了我的销售导师。

00:57:53.600 --> 00:58:12.920
<v Ira Glass>Manny's kind of an unusual car salesman because he has no affection for cars at all. If a customer asks him how much horsepower a car has or how quickly it can go from 0 to 60, usually he has to look up the answer or go ask a coworker. He has no interest in that stuff. So instead, Manny developed a way to sell the customer using a different approach.
<v Ira Glass>曼尼是一个不寻常的汽车销售员，因为他根本对车没感情。如果客户问他一辆车有多少马力，或者它从0加速到60需要多快，通常他不得不查答案或问同事。他对那些东西没兴趣。所以相反，曼尼开发了一种使用不同方法销售给客户的方式。

00:58:12.920 --> 00:58:18.710
<v Manny Rosales>The psychology. How you penetrate his brain.
<v Manny Rosales>心理学。如何渗透他的大脑。

00:58:18.710 --> 00:58:22.760
<v Film Narrator>Sun Tzu says all warfare is deception.
<v Film Narrator>孙武说兵者诡道也。

00:58:22.760 --> 00:58:41.490
<v Brian Reed>To help me understand this-- how to penetrate the brain of one's opponent-- Manny showed me a couple of movies on YouTube. One was a re-enactment of the Second Punic War in the third century BC, Hannibal's campaign against the Romans. Manny wanted me to pay attention to one battle in particular from that war, the Battle of Cannae.
<v Brian Reed>为了帮我理解这个——如何渗透对手的大脑——曼尼在YouTube上给我看了几部电影。其中一部是公元前三世纪第二次布匿战争的重现，汉尼拔对罗马的战役。曼尼要我注意那场战争中的一场特定战斗，坎尼战役。

00:58:41.490 --> 00:58:45.160
<v Film Narrator>One of the bloodiest ever fought.
<v Film Narrator>最血腥的一场。

00:58:45.160 --> 00:58:53.230
<v Brian Reed>At Cannae, Hannibal's troops were vastly outnumbered. He had about 40,000 men versus Rome's army, which had roughly 85,000.
<v Brian Reed>在坎尼，汉尼拔的军队在数量上处于劣势。他有约40,000人，而罗马军队大约有85,000人。

00:58:53.230 --> 00:59:02.910
<v Hannibal>The Romans will have the confidence that comes only with greater numbers. We'll present them with the unexpected and watch them fall apart.
<v Hannibal>罗马人会拥有只有巨大数量才能带来的自信。我们将向他们展示意想不到的东西，看着他们崩溃。

00:59:02.910 --> 00:59:05.830
<v Manny Rosales>See, that is the secret. Present the unexpected.
<v Manny Rosales>看，这就是秘诀。呈现意想不到的东西。

00:59:05.830 --> 00:59:08.130
<v Hannibal>Our forces will be arranged like this.
<v Hannibal>我们的力量将这样排列。

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:21.940
<v Brian Reed>The details of Hannibal's strategy are a bit complicated to go into here. Manny had to draw me a diagram to help me get it. But let's just say it involves an unusual troop formation that made the Romans think they were gaining ground when really they were at Hannibal's whim.
<v Brian Reed>汉尼拔战略的细节有点复杂，这里就不详细讲了。曼尼不得不画一个图表来帮我理解。但我们只说它涉及一种不寻常的部队编队，让罗马人以为他们正在取得进展，而实际上他们完全在汉尼拔的掌握中。

00:59:21.940 --> 00:59:29.130
<v Hannibal>At this point, the Romans will think they have won. Let them taste victory.
<v Hannibal>在这一点上，罗马人会以为他们已经赢了。让他们尝尝胜利的滋味。

00:59:29.130 --> 00:59:40.480
<v Brian Reed>We're watching this, remember, on a laptop on the showroom floor. Customers are milling about, checking out the new cars, as Hannibal's mercenaries and cavalry encircle the Romans and trap them for good.
<v Brian Reed>请记住，我们正在展厅地板上的笔记本电脑上观看这个。当汉尼拔的雇佣兵和骑兵包围罗马人并最终困住他们时，客户正在四处走动，检查新车。

00:59:40.480 --> 00:59:42.110
<v Brian Reed>[BATTLE SOUNDS]
<v Brian Reed>[战斗声]

00:59:42.110 --> 00:59:47.480
<v Manny Rosales>And now the killing has started.
<v Manny Rosales>现在杀戮开始了。

00:59:47.480 --> 00:59:48.740
<v Manny Rosales>[BATTLE SOUNDS CONTINUE]
<v Manny Rosales>[战斗声继续]

00:59:48.740 --> 00:59:58.580
<v Brian Reed>So how does Manny use this to sell Dodge Chargers? Well, just like Hannibal would, he presents the customer with the unexpected.
<v Brian Reed>那么曼尼如何利用这个来卖道奇Charger呢？好吧，就像汉尼拔那样，他向客户呈现意想不到的东西。

00:59:58.580 --> 01:00:18.860
<v Brian Reed>What they expect is for the salesman to be pushy and try to coerce them into buying a car at a certain price. So Manny does the opposite. He acts as if he doesn't even want to sell the car. He agrees with the customer, uses a similar tone, repeats words the customer says. If someone comes in and says he's just there to browse, not to buy, that's totally fine, Manny will say, and he'll back off.
<v Brian Reed>他们期望销售员态度强硬，试图强迫他们以特定价格买车。所以曼尼做相反的事。他表现得好像他根本不想卖那辆车。他同意客户的观点，使用相似的语气，重复客户说的话。如果有人进来，说他只是来看看，不是来买的，那完全没问题，曼尼会说，他会退后。

01:00:18.860 --> 01:00:26.150
<v Manny Rosales>The idea is to make them believe that he's doing everything from his point of view. So he's thinking he's playing his game.
<v Manny Rosales>这个想法是让他们相信他正在从他们的观点出发做每件事。所以他觉得他在玩他的游戏。

01:00:26.150 --> 01:00:27.860
<v Brian Reed>But he's really not?
<v Brian Reed>但他实际上不是？

01:00:27.860 --> 01:00:29.130
<v Manny Rosales>Absolutely not.
<v Manny Rosales>绝对不是。

01:00:29.130 --> 01:00:31.100
<v Brian Reed>What game is he playing in reality?
<v Brian Reed>他实际上在玩什么游戏？

01:00:31.100 --> 01:00:39.690
<v Manny Rosales>My game. Simple. Simple.
<v Manny Rosales>我的游戏。简单。简单。

01:00:39.690 --> 01:01:02.940
<v Brian Reed>Manny gave me an example of this that he'd executed a few days earlier with a customer named Julio, who was looking for a Jeep Grand Cherokee. Julio runs warehouses for a living and negotiates for his company, so Manny knew he'd be a formidable opponent. Julio told me later that he'd already visited seven other Jeep dealerships by the time he got to Town & Country and was calling and texting with another three.
<v Brian Reed>曼尼给我举了个例子，那是他几天前和一个叫胡里奥的客户执行的，胡里奥正在找一辆Jeep大切诺基。胡里奥以管理仓库为生，并为他的公司进行谈判，所以曼尼知道他会是一个强大的对手。胡里奥后来告诉我，当他到达Town & Country时，他已经访问了其他七家Jeep经销店，并正在和另外三家电话和短信联系。

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:21.140
<v Brian Reed>When he sat down across from Manny, Julio made the first offer, $36,000. The Jeep was listed at $45,000, so Julio's offer was absurdly low. Julio knew it was unrealistic. He expected Manny to say no way, that he could never give it to him for that price.
<v Brian Reed>当他坐在曼尼对面时，胡里奥提出了第一个报价，36,000美元。Jeep的标价是45,000美元，所以胡里奥的报价荒谬地低。胡里奥知道这不切实际。他期望曼尼说没门，说他绝不可能以那个价格卖给他。

01:01:21.140 --> 01:01:23.000
<v Brian Reed>But then listen to what Manny did instead.
<v Brian Reed>但听听曼尼反而做了什么。

01:01:23.000 --> 01:01:28.540
<v Manny Rosales>I said, all right. If I can sell it to you for $36,000, are you ready to do it right now? Show me the money. Where's the credit card?
<v Manny Rosales>我说，好的。如果我能以36,000美元卖给你，你准备好现在就做了吗？给我钱。信用卡在哪？

01:01:28.540 --> 01:01:34.460
<v Brian Reed>Where's the credit card. Manny says this was the key move, the unexpected move.
<v Brian Reed>信用卡在哪。曼尼说这是关键动作，意想不到的动作。

01:01:34.460 --> 01:01:48.250
<v Manny Rosales>Because they never thought I would say yes. Suddenly, they don't know what to do or what to say. They get confused. He cannot back up because that's what he said to me. If you give me $36,000, I'll buy the car.
<v Manny Rosales>因为他们从没想过我会说好。突然，他们不知道该做什么或说什么。他们被搞糊涂了。他不能退缩，因为那是他对我说的话。如果你给我36,000美元，我就买这辆车。

01:01:48.250 --> 01:01:48.660
<v Manny Rosales>[BATTLE SOUNDS]
<v Manny Rosales>[战斗声]

01:01:48.660 --> 01:01:54.260
<v Hannibal>At this point, the Romans will think they have won. Let them taste victory.
<v Hannibal>在这一点上，罗马人会以为他们已经赢了。让他们尝尝胜利的滋味。

01:01:54.260 --> 01:01:59.150
<v Brian Reed>So he pulled his credit card out?
<v Brian Reed>所以他掏出了信用卡？

01:01:59.150 --> 01:02:06.050
<v Manny Rosales>Absolutely. He gave it to me. And I said, all right, let me take the offer to my manager, even though I knew that we cannot do it.
<v Manny Rosales>绝对。他给了我。然后我说，好的，让我把报价带给我的经理，尽管我知道我们做不到。

01:02:06.050 --> 01:02:30.590
<v Brian Reed>After days of negotiation, Manny ended up scoring this deal. Julio paid $40,000 for the Jeep, which was low. The dealership lost money on the car. But after it was all over, Julio told me that this move, when Manny pretended to take his low offer seriously, he said, without that, he would have walked out of Town & Country immediately, which would have meant no sale, nothing towards the monthly quota.
<v Brian Reed>经过几天的谈判，曼尼最终达成了这笔交易。胡里奥为这辆Jeep支付了40,000美元，这很低。经销店在这辆车上亏了钱。但在这一切结束后，胡里奥告诉我，当曼尼假装认真对待他的低价时，他那个动作，他说，如果没有那个，他会立即离开Town & Country，这意味着没交易，对月度配额没贡献。

01:02:30.590 --> 01:02:48.170
<v Ira Glass>By 4:00 PM, the hustling in the showroom is paying off. Chips are going up on the board in Freddie's office. Even one of the guys in the back rooms is pitching in-- a finance guy, Terrance Kelly, TK. He's reaching for the phone.
<v Ira Glass>下午4点，展厅里的奔波正在得到回报。筹码正在弗雷迪办公室的白板上增加。即使后台房间里的家伙之一也在帮忙——财务员特伦斯·凯利，TK。他正在伸手去拿电话。

01:02:48.170 --> 01:03:00.400
<v Terrance Kelly>So basically, the numbers are down. They need all the deals. So they made me call my niece in Las Vegas, believe it or not. She's been trying to buy a car anyway.
<v Terrance Kelly>所以基本上，数字下降了。他们需要所有的交易。所以他们让我打电话给我在拉斯维加斯的侄女，信不信由你。她反正一直想买车。

01:03:00.400 --> 01:03:01.600
<v Terrance Kelly>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Terrance Kelly>[电话铃声]

01:03:01.600 --> 01:03:02.720
<v Katie>Hello?
<v Katie>喂？

01:03:02.720 --> 01:03:05.500
<v Terrance Kelly>Hi, Katie.
<v Terrance Kelly>嗨，凯蒂。

01:03:05.500 --> 01:03:06.510
<v Katie>Hey.
<v Katie>嘿。

01:03:06.510 --> 01:03:09.720
<v Terrance Kelly>How are you? I have you on the speakerphone.
<v Terrance Kelly>你好吗？我在用免提。

01:03:09.720 --> 01:03:10.830
<v Katie>OK.
<v Katie>好的。

01:03:10.830 --> 01:03:29.570
<v Ira Glass>TK's got 34 nieces and nephews. Katie is his goddaughter, though, so he keeps a special eye out for her. At the time that this was recorded, the rest of the family didn't know that she was pregnant, though he did. Even though he did not know, until he picked up the receiver and started filling out the forms for the car sale, her fiance's last name.
<v Ira Glass>TK有34个侄子和侄女。不过凯蒂是他的教女，所以他特别关照她。录制这段时，家里其他人还不知道她怀孕了，尽管他知道。即使他不知道，直到他拿起接收器开始为卖车填写表格，她未婚夫的姓氏。

01:03:29.570 --> 01:03:45.570
<v Terrance Kelly>OK. Charles Parker? You're getting married to somebody with the last name of Parker? Oh, Barker. Oh. Is he related to Bob at all?
<v Terrance Kelly>OK。查尔斯·帕克？你要嫁给姓帕克的人？噢，巴克。噢。鲍勃和他是亲戚吗？

01:03:45.570 --> 01:04:00.500
<v Terrance Kelly>What does he do, by the way? I never asked you. Ooh. HVAC company? Nice. I like that. Well, as long as he's happy. You're in the right area for air conditioning, I guess.
<v Terrance Kelly>顺便问一下，他是做什么的？我从没问过你。噢。暖通空调公司？不错。我喜欢那个。好吧，只要他开心。我想你在空调方面处于合适的领域。

01:04:00.500 --> 01:04:18.790
<v Ira Glass>The dealership is selling the car to Katie below cost at a $900 loss. And TK is going to end up spending $1,200 of his own money to ship the car out to her in Las Vegas. And this? This is the sale that puts him over the top. This is the 129th sale of the month.
<v Ira Glass>经销店以低于成本价卖给凯蒂，亏损900美元。而TK最终要自掏腰包花1,200美元把车运到拉斯维加斯给她。而这个？这是让她跨越终点的销售。这是当月的第129笔销售。

01:04:18.790 --> 01:04:27.730
<v Ira Glass>Just a few minutes before 5 o'clock, right after this happens, I stop by Freddie the general manager's office. Nobody is running into high fives. There's not even a staff announcement on the PA saying they made it.
<v Ira Glass>5点前几分钟，就在这事发生后，我顺便去了总经理弗雷迪的办公室。没有人跑着击掌。甚至没有PA系统向全体员工广播他们达成了目标。

01:04:27.730 --> 01:04:31.050
<v Ira Glass>I've got to say, it's weirdly anticlimactic.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，这非常虎头蛇尾。

01:04:31.050 --> 01:04:39.620
<v Freddie Hoyt>There's no hurrah. There's no balloons falling from the ceiling. We did our job. Everybody earned their paycheck. That's the bottom line.
<v Freddie Hoyt>没有欢呼。没有气球从天花板掉下来。我们完成了工作。每个人都赚到了工资。这就是底线。

01:04:39.620 --> 01:04:43.220
<v Ira Glass>And then the clock starts ticking tomorrow morning on next month?
<v Ira Glass>然后明天早上时间就开始计算下个月了？

01:04:43.220 --> 01:04:58.670
<v Freddie Hoyt>We're all a bunch of losers tomorrow morning. You start all over again. That's how it goes. Oh, the wife. Oh, my god. Don't get married.
<v Freddie Hoyt>明天早上我们都是失败者。你得从头再来。就是这样。噢，妻子。噢，天哪。千万别结婚。

01:04:58.670 --> 01:05:07.240
<v Freddie Hoyt>We're fighting because I told-- she says to me, what time are you getting home? I said, what time do I usually get home at the end of the month? Whenever I get home. What, do you want a time card? What, am I in punching in?
<v Freddie Hoyt>我们在吵架，因为我告诉——她对我说，你几点回家？我说，我在月底通常几点回家？我什么时候回家，我就什么时候回家。怎么，你想要一张考勤卡？怎么，我在打卡？

01:05:07.240 --> 01:05:18.020
<v Freddie Hoyt>I said, when I get home, I get home, and I hung up on her. So now she's pissed and she's texting. "OK, so why do you have to bite my head off?" I told her she should've married a plumber. 9:00 to 5:00, OK? Weekends off, OK?
<v Freddie Hoyt>我说，我到家的时候我就到了，然后我挂了她电话。所以现在她气疯了，在发短信。“OK，那你为什么要对我大吼大叫？”我告诉她她本该嫁给水管工。朝九晚五，OK？周末休息，OK？

01:05:18.020 --> 01:05:31.720
<v Ira Glass>He texts her back. This fight actually continues for the next few hours over text message. Then, at 6:57, two hours before closing, one of the managers, Mike Perez, and Jason start huddling the desk, a little freaked out.
<v Ira Glass>他发短信回给她。这场争吵实际上通过短信持续了接下来的几个小时。然后，在6点57分，关闭前两个小时，经理之一迈克·佩雷斯和杰森开始在服务台挤在一起，有点吓坏了。

01:05:31.720 --> 01:05:39.730
<v Ira Glass>Mike walks to Freddie's office to explain the problem to Freddie. Their count is wrong. They're not at 129 because there's an extra chip on the board.
<v Ira Glass>迈克走到弗雷迪的办公室向弗雷迪解释问题。他们的计数错了。他们没到129，因为板上多了一个筹码。

01:05:39.730 --> 01:05:42.020
<v Mike Perez>There was a chip up here that was supposed to be pulled off.
<v Mike Perez>这儿有一个筹码本该拿掉的。

01:05:42.020 --> 01:05:43.520
<v Freddie Hoyt>What do you mean? We're down one?
<v Freddie Hoyt>你是什么意思？我们少了一辆？

01:05:43.520 --> 01:05:49.650
<v Mike Perez>Yeah. This was up there. It should have been off of there because the customer switched cars. But someone put the new chip up but didn't take the old chip off.
<v Mike Perez>是的。这上面有。它本该拿掉，因为客户换车了。但有人放了新筹码上去，却没把旧筹码拿掉。

01:05:49.650 --> 01:05:51.740
<v Freddie Hoyt>So I'm still at 128?
<v Freddie Hoyt>所以我还在128？

01:05:51.740 --> 01:06:04.750
<v Ira Glass>Freddie does know that, at this moment, there is another deal being negotiated that could possibly get them back up to 129. Peter is the salesman for that one. But the deal is stuck over the price of the customer's trade-in, what they should pay for it.
<v Ira Glass>弗雷迪知道，在这一刻，有另一笔交易正在谈判中，可能让他们回到129。彼得是那一笔的销售员。但交易卡在客户的旧车抵价价格上，他们应该付多少。

01:06:04.750 --> 01:06:07.710
<v Freddie Hoyt>Go make that deal right now. Whatever it is, just make the fucking deal.
<v Freddie Hoyt>现在就去做那笔交易。无论是什么，就做那该死的交易。

01:06:07.710 --> 01:06:30.490
<v Ira Glass>But that deal is not too easy to settle. And while they're waiting, more bad news. You remember that red Grand Cherokee that Scott and Mike Lester each had sold to different people and then Mike Lester's guy dropped out and Scott got the deal, and there was the kiss? Now Scott's guy also drops out. He doesn't want to buy tonight because he wants his mom to see the car tomorrow.
<v Ira Glass>但那笔交易并不太容易解决。而在他们等待时，更多坏消息。你还记得那辆红色的大切诺基，斯科蒂和迈克·莱斯特每人把它卖给不同的人，然后迈克·莱斯特的家伙退出了，斯科蒂得到了交易，还有那个吻？现在斯科蒂的家伙也退出了。他今晚不想买，因为他想让妈妈明天看看车。

01:06:30.490 --> 01:06:32.600
<v Ira Glass>Freddie overhears Scott saying this to one of the managers.
<v Ira Glass>弗雷迪听见斯科蒂对经理之一说这话。

01:06:32.600 --> 01:06:36.050
<v Freddie Hoyt>What, you have a problem? What's the matter with it? What's the matter with it, Scotty?
<v Freddie Hoyt>什么，你有问题吗？它有什么问题？斯科蒂，它有什么问题？

01:06:36.050 --> 01:06:39.600
<v Scott Froehlich>The guy wants to show his mom tomorrow the car. Originally--
<v Scott Froehlich>那家伙想让妈妈明天看车。最初——

01:06:39.600 --> 01:06:42.090
<v Freddie Hoyt>What do you mean? This isn't a deal?
<v Freddie Hoyt>你是什么意思？这不是交易？

01:06:42.090 --> 01:06:45.860
<v Scott Froehlich>I thought it was, but--
<v Scott Froehlich>我以为是，但——

01:06:45.860 --> 01:06:50.420
<v Freddie Hoyt>Are you fucking kidding me? You have insurance on this car and everything?
<v Freddie Hoyt>你在跟我开玩笑吗？你这辆车有保险和一切吗？

01:06:50.420 --> 01:06:55.180
<v Scott Froehlich>I don't even-- he didn't even send it into the bank because we don't have his prior employer.
<v Scott Froehlich>我甚至不——他甚至没寄给银行，因为我们没有他之前雇主的信息。

01:06:55.180 --> 01:07:02.660
<v Freddie Hoyt>What are we doing? I thought these all went into the bank today! I asked you, all the cars have insurance and went into the bank? You've got to be fucking kidding me!
<v Freddie Hoyt>我们在做什么？我以为这些今天都进银行了！我问过你，所有的车都有保险并进银行了吗？你在跟我开玩笑！

01:07:02.660 --> 01:07:08.570
<v Ira Glass>Scott's deal is dead. They're at 127 with just two hours left before closing.
<v Ira Glass>斯科蒂的交易死了。他们在距离关闭只有两个小时的时候是127。

01:07:08.570 --> 01:07:11.450
<v Freddie Hoyt>I'm fucked. I just lost Scott's. I'm fucked.
<v Freddie Hoyt>我完了。我刚输掉了斯科蒂的那笔。我完了。

01:07:11.450 --> 01:07:25.370
<v Ira Glass>So now they scramble to figure out, OK, there was Mike Lester's customer for that red Grand Cherokee. Could they possibly give him better terms that would bring him back to the table? Freddie orders one of the managers, Mike Perez, to make that happen.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在他们争先恐后地弄清楚，OK，有迈克·莱斯特的那个红色大切诺基的客户。他们能否提供更好的条款，让他回到谈判桌？弗雷迪命令经理之一迈克·佩雷斯实现这一目标。

01:07:25.370 --> 01:07:35.620
<v Freddie Hoyt>Just do it. It's going to be too late. You're going to wait a half hour longer. It's getting later and later. I'd rather have a sure thing than not. Just fucking do it. How much does that lose?
<v Freddie Hoyt>去做吧。太晚了。你要再等半小时。时间越来越晚了。我宁愿要确定的东西而不是没有。就做吧。那笔交易亏多少？

01:07:35.620 --> 01:07:56.200
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. The only question is, how much does the deal lose, not whether it loses. So Mike Perez calls Richie in finance. Richie tells him he's in luck. The guy actually called back saying that he would agree to the terms but-- OK, here's the catch-- he just wants 24 hours to think about this. Mike Perez asks, OK, if we sweeten the terms, can we get him to decide right now? Rich doesn't think so.
<v Ira Glass>是的。唯一的问题是，交易亏多少，而不是是否亏钱。所以迈克·佩雷斯给财务部的里奇打电话。里奇告诉他他走运了。那家伙竟然回电话说他同意条款，但——OK，有个陷阱——他只想24小时考虑一下。迈克·佩雷斯问，OK，如果我们提供更好的条款，能让他现在就决定吗？里奇觉得不行。

01:07:56.200 --> 01:08:01.500
<v Rich>It's not going to change anything tonight. For me to pull down my pants, you don't need to.
<v Rich>今晚什么都不会改变。我不需要脱裤子。

01:08:01.500 --> 01:08:06.250
<v Mike Perez>But Freddie's telling me to. Literally, he's telling me to, so I need to just--
<v Mike Perez>但弗雷迪在叫我。他简直是在叫我，所以我需要——

01:08:06.250 --> 01:08:20.030
<v Rich>Bro, whatever you need to do. But I'm telling you, if I told him it was free and the same, you're going to get the same answer anyway-- 24 hours.
<v Rich>兄弟，无论你需要做什么。但我告诉你，如果我告诉他是免费的，一样的，你还是会得到同样的回答——24小时。

01:08:20.030 --> 01:08:22.940
<v Ira Glass>All is lost.
<v Ira Glass>全完了。

01:08:22.940 --> 01:08:46.280
<v Ira Glass>And then, in just six minutes, they turn it around. Rich offers a better deal to that guy on the red Grand Cherokee after all and yes, he takes it. And Peter's deal that was stuck over the price of the trade-in gets unstuck when the managers suck it up and pay $1,500 more for the trade-in than they think it's worth. Back to 129.
<v Ira Glass>然后，在短短六分钟内，他们扭转了局面。里奇毕竟向红色大切诺基的那个家伙提供了更好的交易，是的，他接受了。而彼得卡在抵价上的交易当经理们咬牙支付比他们认为值钱的还要多1,500美元来抵价时，被解开了。回到129。

01:08:46.280 --> 01:08:59.360
<v Ira Glass>Everybody seems a little stunned by what just happened when it's done. Freddie and Sal look out at the showroom from the desk like old sailors on the deck of a 19th century ship that just got thrashed around by a hurricane and also a murderous white whale.
<v Ira Glass>事情结束时，每个人似乎都被刚才发生的事情弄得有点呆住了。弗雷迪和萨尔像19世纪帆船甲板上的老水手一样从服务台望向展厅，那艘船刚被飓风和一条凶残的白鲸摧毁。

01:08:59.360 --> 01:09:04.690
<v Freddie Hoyt>Do you believe this shit, Sal? I feel like back in fucking Infiniti days.
<v Freddie Hoyt>萨尔，你相信这该死的事吗？我觉得回到了英菲尼迪的日子。

01:09:04.690 --> 01:09:05.850
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Oh, my god.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>噢，天哪。

01:09:05.850 --> 01:09:08.400
<v Freddie Hoyt>This is ridiculous, this month.
<v Freddie Hoyt>这个月真是荒谬。

01:09:08.400 --> 01:09:10.250
<v Sal Lanzilotta>Oh, my god.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>噢，天哪。

01:09:10.250 --> 01:09:12.070
<v Freddie Hoyt>This is just--
<v Freddie Hoyt>这只是——

01:09:12.070 --> 01:09:16.450
<v Sal Lanzilotta>The month wasn't bad enough. We've got to suffer a little more the last day of the month.
<v Sal Lanzilotta>这个月还不够糟糕。我们还得在最后一天受点苦。

01:09:16.450 --> 01:09:18.620
<v Freddie Hoyt>Un-fucking-believable. Fuck me.
<v Freddie Hoyt>真他妈的不真实。去他妈的。

01:09:18.620 --> 01:09:23.910
<v Ira Glass>Freddie's phone, of course, doesn't stop with the text messages through all this.
<v Ira Glass>弗雷迪的手机，当然，在这一过程中没停止收到短信。

01:09:23.910 --> 01:09:32.910
<v Freddie Hoyt>[SIGHS] God damn. The wife, who else? I don't know what she's even fighting for.
<v Freddie Hoyt>[叹气] 该死。妻子，还能是谁？我都不知道她在为打什么。

01:09:32.910 --> 01:09:53.480
<v Ira      Glass>They get their bonus from Chrysler, $68,000. And because of it, the dealership ends up in the black, not the red, for the month. Jason finishes the month at 29 cars, almost twice anyone else, as usual. Manny has 10; Mike L, 15; Scott, 18.
<v Ira      Glass>他们从克莱斯勒拿到了奖金，68,000美元。正因为如此，经销店当月是以盈利而不是亏损结束的。杰森本月以29辆车结束，像往常一样，几乎是其他人的两倍。曼尼10辆；迈克·L，15辆；斯科蒂，18辆。

01:09:53.480 --> 01:10:04.220
<v Ira      Glass>The used car side, Joe Monti's side, did not meet its goal. They were hoping for 70. They got 57, meaning Joe Monti probably will not be getting his year-end bonus this year.
<v Ira      Glass>二手车方，乔·蒙蒂方，没达到目标。他们希望能有70辆。他们得到了57辆，这意味着乔·蒙蒂今年可能拿不到年终奖了。

01:10:04.220 --> 01:10:11.080
<v Ira      Glass>And Bob T, the salesman at the bottom of the board with the least sales this month, well, the night isn't over for Bob T.
<v Ira      Glass>还有鲍勃·T，那个在这个月销售额垫底的销售员，好吧，鲍勃·T的夜晚还没结束。

01:10:11.080 --> 01:10:15.280
<v Bob Tantillo>Where'd you put the key? Where'd you put the car? I've got the key. Where's the car?
<v Bob Tantillo>你把钥匙放哪了？车放哪了？我有钥匙。车呢？

01:10:15.280 --> 01:10:34.340
<v Ira Glass>Not long after they reached 129, a couple walks in wanting to buy a Dodge Dart for their 19-year-old son. Bob T rushes them through the test drive, has the husband sit at his computer and use his phone to quickly call the insurance company and then runs-- literally runs-- to get their new car prepped and inspected in the garage.
<v Ira Glass>他们达到129后不久，一对夫妇走进来想给他们19岁的儿子买辆道奇Dart。鲍勃·T催促他们完成试驾，让丈夫坐在他的电脑前用手机快速给保险公司打电话，然后跑——literally跑——去把他们的车在车库里预备好并检查。

01:10:34.340 --> 01:10:36.170
<v Speaker 1>Bob, is this one going tonight?
<v Speaker 1>鲍勃，这一辆今晚要出去吗？

01:10:36.170 --> 01:10:38.040
<v Bob Tantillo>He says to bring it right over to service.
<v Bob Tantillo>他说马上送到维修部。

01:10:38.040 --> 01:10:40.000
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, but is it going, or is it a paper delivery?
<v Speaker 1>是的，但这在走吗，还是纸面交付？

01:10:40.000 --> 01:10:41.540
<v Bob Tantillo>I don't know if it's going.
<v Bob Tantillo>我不知道它走不走。

01:10:41.540 --> 01:10:42.230
<v Speaker 2>Find out.
<v Speaker 2>弄清楚。

01:10:42.230 --> 01:10:43.370
<v Bob Tantillo>It should go.
<v Bob Tantillo>它应该走的。

01:10:43.370 --> 01:10:50.550
<v Speaker 1>Who the fuck wants to take a car home at 10 o'clock at night? You still got the receipt for them? Bring them back!
<v Speaker 1>谁他妈想在晚上10点把车带回家？你还有他们的收据吗？把他们带回来！

01:10:50.550 --> 01:10:59.830
<v Bob Tantillo>These guys could say what they want. This old timer, nobody works as fast as me here. You notice that? Huh? Yes or no?
<v Bob Tantillo>这些家伙可以想说什么说什么。这个老骨头，这里没人比我干得快。你注意到了吗？嗯？是还是不是？

01:10:59.830 --> 01:11:53.340
<v Ira Glass>And with that, Bob T lands Town & Country's last sale of the month, number 130, one car past the goal of 129. Or anyway, that's what they thought that night. The next morning, when everybody comes in, they look at Chrysler's official count on the computer. And according to Chrysler, no, their count was wrong. One of their sales should not have been counted in October, which means that they did not have 130 cars like they thought. They had only 129, exactly 129. Which means, yes, it was Bob T and his very last sale of the month that took them across the finish line. Bob T saved the day.
<v Ira Glass>就这样，鲍勃·T拿下了Town & Country该月的最后一笔销售，第130辆，超过了129的目标。或者说，那天晚上他们是这么想的。第二天早上，当每个人进来时，他们看电脑上克莱斯勒的官方统计。根据克莱斯勒的统计，不，他们的计数错了。其中一笔销售不该计入十月，这意味着他们没像自己认为的那样卖出130辆。他们只有129辆，整整129辆。这意味着，是的，正是鲍勃·T和他当月的最后一笔销售带他们冲过了终点线。鲍勃·T拯救了这一天。

01:11:53.340 --> 01:12:15.590
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks to Marc Brodlieb and the entire staff at Town & Country Jeep Chrysler Dodge Ram in Levittown for letting us hang out for days and barely ever suggesting during that time that one of us might help them reach their goal for the month by buying a new Grand Cherokee. We sent a photographer who does celebrity photographs-- his name is Jeff Minton-- out to Levittown to photograph the sales staff at Town & Country. You can see the results at our website, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢马里·布罗德利布和Town & Country Jeep Chrysler Dodge Ram（莱维敦）的全体员工，让我们呆了几天，在此期间几乎从未暗示过我们中的某人可以通过买一辆新大切诺基来帮助他们达到月度目标。我们派了一位专门拍摄名人的摄影师——他的名字叫杰夫·明顿——去莱维敦拍摄Town & Country的销售人员。你可以在我们的网站thisamericanlife.org看到结果。

01:12:15.590 --> 01:12:30.300
<v Ira Glass>Today's program was produced by Robyn Semien and myself with Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Sarah Koenig, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer for this episode was Julie Snyder. Production help from BA Parker and Diane Wu. Matt Tierney is our technical director.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目由罗宾·塞米恩和我本人制作，亚历克斯·布隆伯格、本·卡尔霍恩、莎拉·柯尼格、米基·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、布莱恩·里德、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克参与。本集的资深制片人是朱莉·斯奈德。制作协助来自BA帕克和戴安·吴。马特·蒂尔尼是我们的技术总监。

01:12:30.300 --> 01:12:45.910
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. On a vacation this month in Rome, he stood in line for three hours-- three hours-- at the Colosseum then gave up, went back to his hotel.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由PRX（公共广播交易所）分发给公共广播电台。和往常一样，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生。本月在罗马度假时，他在斗兽场排了三个小时——三个小时——然后放弃了，回到了酒店。

01:12:45.910 --> 01:12:53.410
<v Hannibal>At this point, the Romans will think they have won. Let them taste victory.
<v Hannibal>在这一点上，罗马人会以为他们已经赢了。让他们尝尝胜利的滋味。

01:12:53.410 --> 01:12:58.410
<v Ira Glass>Don't worry. He'll be back. I'm Ira Glass. We will also be back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>别担心。他会回来的。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们下周会带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
